INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6124,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11449633','Relationships between regional burden of tau pathology and age at death and disease duration in PSP.','Badihian N, Tosakulwong N, Weigand SD, Ali F, Clark HM, Stierwalt J, Botha H, Savica R, Dickson DW, Whitwell JL, Josephs KA.','2024','globose pallidum akinesia globose oligodendroglia kaj spsp spsp jacksonville saccadic psis asrs neurohistopathological cerebrum precentral mammillary commissure colliculus rd4 cp13 phosphoserine globose cp13 precentral tegmentum semiquantitatively penalization brms psis psprs psprs saccadic asrs psprs psprs midlines asrs esup precentral precentral scatterplots 7unit precentral tegmentum precentral tegmentum scatterplots globose varius bindings pallidum fasciculus peduncle oligodendroglia perineuronal buffering butyric neurotrophins pathogeneses amnesia nihms2021851 nihms2021851 ns89757 nidcd dc12519 dc14942 litvan agid calne duvoisin golbe grafman growdon hallett jankovic tolosa olszewski spsp roemer revesz ghayal rainwater hoglinger litvan mandrekar lukic respondek compta rajput troakes swieten giese roeber herms hoglinger machulda utianski botha spychalla golbe ohman beisser elghoul avula edmonson lezak howieson loring hannay utianski ironside ansorge baborie bieniek bodi feany gentleman giaccone hatanpaa heale hortobagyi kofler kovari kril matej milenkovic rozemuller seilhean tagliavini takao tolnay woulfe ruberu sawabe kakuta yamanouchi sarraj bodi bogdanovic bugiani gentleman giaccone ironside kavantzas korkolopoulou meyronet monoranu parkkinen patsouris roggendorf rozemuller stadelmann nessler streichenberger brainnet buciuc botha utianski machulda ahlskog fcac108 gelman burkner brms stan botha utianski piggott neurochemistry sjostrom surova granberg westman westen svenningsson ntt arani tsujimura ishizuka hamdy botha ntt ahlskog nath shlomo olszewski geda nihms2021851 nihms2021851','Abstract Background: A definitive diagnosis of progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) can only be established through neuropathological evaluations where cardinal tau lesions are identified. Relationships between regional tau burden and disease duration/age at death is unclear. Objective: To investigate relationships between tau burden in different brain regions and disease duration and age at death in PSP and determine whether association are influenced by PSP subtype (subcortical/cortical) or co-pathologies. Methods: We identified 45 PSP patients with PSP who were evaluated at Mayo Clinic between 2009–2023, died and underwent histopathological evaluation. We performed semi-quantitative lesion count for each of four cardinal lesions (globose neurofibrillary tangles, threads, tufted astrocytes, and coiled bodies) across 10 brain regions. We fit Bayesian linear hierarchical regression models to estimate the relationship between total pathological burden, and disease duration and age at death by region and the influence of subtype and co-pathologies. Results: Of the 45 patients, 18 (40%) were female. Median age at death was 75 (56–87) years and median disease duration was 8 (3,15) years. Younger age at death was associated with greater total tau burden in the pallidum, red nucleus, striatum, and subthalamic nucleus (all p≤0.01). Shorter disease duration was associated with greater total tau burden in the red nucleus (p=0.05). There was no evidence for a difference in association between lesion types. Furthermore, PSP subtype and co-pathologies did not influence associations. Conclusions: The findings from this study suggest that age and disease duration influence burden of tau pathology in subcortical regions in PSP. Introduction Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) is a rare neurodegenerative disorder that presents with ocular motor dysfunction associated with postural instability, akinesia, and/or cognitive dysfunction[ 1 ]. It is caused by the intracerebral aggregation of the microtubule-associated protein tau and is a four-repeat (4R) tauopathy. A definitive diagnosis of PSP can only be established through neuropathological evaluation. The presence of four cardinal tau immunoreactive lesions that affect neurons, astrocytes and oligodendrocytes in specific brain regions help to confirm the diagnosis of PSP. These cardinal lesions are globose neurofibrillary tangles, threads, tufted astrocytes, and coiled bodies[ 2 ]. Median duration of survival in PSP is approximately 8 years[ 3 ]. Early development of cognitive impairment, dementia, falls, and dysphagia are clinical predictors of poor survival in patients with PSP[ 3 ]. Previous studies have reported a relationship between higher glial tau burden and shorter disease duration in PSP [ 4 , 5 ]. However, little is known about the relationship between tau burden in different brain regions and disease duration or age at death. Understanding these relationships would help us in developing more accurate biomarkers for defining the prognosis and developing targeted medications. In the present study, we aim to investigate the relationship between the regional burden of tau pathology in neurons, astrocytes, and oligodendroglia, and disease duration and age at death in patients with PSP. We also assess whether co-pathologies play any role in these associations. We hypothesized that there should be an inverse relationship between the burden of tau pathology in different brain regions and patients’ disease duration and age at death. Materials and Methods: Recruitment of patients with PSP The Neurodegenerative Research Group (NRG) at Mayo Clinic, Minnesota, recruited patients clinically diagnosed with possible or probable PSP from September 2009 to September 2023 and prospectively followed them. Board-certified neurologists with expertise in PSP (KAJ and FA), utilized accepted diagnostic criteria at the time of evaluation to render the most appropriate diagnosis and recorded patients’ clinical characteristics, symptom onset and disease course. From September 2009 to June 2017, the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke and the Society for PSP (NINDS-SPSP) criteria were used for clinical diagnosis, switching to the Movement Disorders Society-PSP (MDS-PSP) criteria from July 2017 to September 2023. To ensure consistency, patient records initially evaluated using NINDS-SPSP [ 1 ] were retrospectively reviewed to align with the MDS-PSP criteria [ 6 ]. The brains of individuals with a written informed consent were removed after death and sent to Mayo Clinic, Jacksonville, Florida for standardized histopathological evaluation based on the accepted published methodologies [ 7 ]. In the present study, we included only those patients who received a definite diagnosis of PSP after histopathological evaluations[ 2 ]. Age at symptom onset was defined as the first instance that the patient experienced a neurological, cognitive, or behavioral deficit that worsened over time and had no other identifiable causes[ 3 ]. Disease duration was calculated as the time from symptom onset to death. We further categorized patients into two groups: PSP-Cortical (the included subtypes were PSP with predominant corticobasal syndrome, PSP with predominant speech-language disorder, and PSP with predominant frontal presentation) and PSP-Subcortical (including PSP subtypes of Richardson syndrome (RS), progressive gait freezing and Parkinsonism). The Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) was used to evaluate overall cognitive function. The overall severity of the disease was assessed using the PSP-rating scale, in conjunction with its gait/midline subscale (G/M), which assesses impairments in gait and posture [ 8 ]. The PSP Saccadic Impairment Scale (PSIS) was employed to evaluate abnormalities in ocular movement [ 9 ]. The Frontal Assessment Battery (FAB) was employed for the evaluation of frontal executive functions [ 10 ]. The Movement Disorders Society-Sponsored Revision of the Unified Parkinson’s Disease Rating Scale (UPDRS)-part III was utilized to evaluate extra-pyramidal motor severity. The Apraxia of Speech Rating Scale (ASRS)-Version III was utilized to evaluate apraxia of speech which is now a well-recognized feature of speech and language predominant PSP [ 11 ]. The Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board approved the study. Neurohistopathological Evaluations One brain hemisphere (usually the left) was prepared to obtain brain sections embedded in paraffin and fixed in formalin. The other hemisphere was frozen for future genetic/biochemical research. We performed coronal cuts of the cerebrum to evaluate the precentral gyrus (located in the posterior frontal lobe), superior frontal gyrus (at the level of the anterior hippocampus), ventral thalamus and subthalamic nucleus (located posterior to the mammillary bodies), and striatum and globus pallidus (at the level of anterior commissure). To assess midbrain, substantia nigra and red nucleus, the superior colliculus was identified, and transverse cuts of the brainstem were made. We also performed transverse cuts from the cerebellum in order to analyze the cerebellar dentate nucleus, and the appropriate location was identified as perpendicular to the long axis of the brainstem. Sections were analyzed using routine Hematoxylin and Eosin, Thioflavin-S, and Gallyas silver staining techniques. A single neuropathologist (D.W.D.) evaluated all cases. To detect 4R tau immunoreactive lesions a 4R tau specific antibody RD4 (CP13; 1:1000; mouse IgG1 to phosphoserine 202, gift from the late Peter Davis, PhD, Feinstein Institute, Long Island, NY) was utilized. A definite diagnosis of PSP was made if globose neurofibrillary tangles, tufted astrocytes, and oligodendroglial coiled bodies were present in key brain regions including the motor cortex, basal ganglia and brainstem[ 2 ]. Additional concurrent pathologies, including aging-related tau astrogliopathy (ARTAG) [ 12 ], argyrophilic grain disease (AGD) [ 13 ], Alzheimer’s disease neuropathologic changes[ 7 ], and Lewy body disease [ 14 ] were also documented. Semiquantitative 4R tau lesion burden We employed anti-CP13 antibody and immunohistochemistry to assess tau lesion count (tau burden) in the following ten brain regions: superior frontal cortex, precentral (motor) cortex, globus pallidus, striatum, subthalamic nucleus, ventral thalamus, red nucleus, substantia nigra, midbrain tegmentum, and cerebellar dentate nucleus. In each region, lesion count was semiquantitatively graded for pre tangles/neurofibrillary tangles, coiled bodies, neuropil threads, and tufted astrocytes based the number of lesions present in each ×200 magnification microscopic visual field. We used the following four-point scale grading system: no lesions=0; few lesions (1–3 lesions)=1; moderate number of lesions (4–6 lesions)=2; many (i.e. severe) lesions (≥7 lesions)=3 [ 4 ]. Representative images for each of these tau lesions has been previously published [ 15 ]. Statistical analysis For statistical analysis, we defined the dependent variable as the numeric sum of the semiquantitative burden scores by summing across the four lesion types within each region for each participant. With ten regions of interest, we analyzed the data in a repeated measures framework with lesion burden as the outcome variable. Our first model used age at death as the primary predictor and we modelled the mean lesion burden as a function of age at death separately by region. To account for the repeated measures design, our model included a region-specific random intercept and a region-specific random slope for age along with a participant-specific intercept. In this way our model could be characterized as a linear mixed effects model or as a hierarchical linear model [ 16 ]. Among the advantages of fitting a single model for each of the ten regions is that this approach addresses issue of multiple comparisons via shrinkage or penalization [ 17 ]. Our second model was based on disease duration at death but was otherwise the same as the age model. Our third and fourth models were based on disease duration and age at death but included separate correlated random intercepts and slopes for the cortical and subcortical subtypes. These mixed effects/hierarchical models allowed us to estimate region-specific associations by subtype while addressing potential overfitting problems or estimation artefacts that can be introduced when performing subset analyses [ 17 ]. We summarized associations as the difference in mean burden for a five-year difference in age at death or a five-year difference in disease duration. Models were fit using R and the brms package [ 18 ] with default priors, five chains, and 10,000 iterations per chain. Lastly, we evaluated the relationship between age at death and disease duration and co-pathologies using logistic regression. Results: Demographics and clinical characteristic From our cohort of 190 PSP patients recruited by the NRG, we identified 45 patients who had died and underwent brain autopsy with a pathological diagnosis of PSP. Demographics and clinical characteristics of patients are presented in Table-1 . Eighteen (40%) were female and 26 (58%) were classified as PSP-subcortical. The median (min, max) age at disease onset was 67 (50, 78) years among all cases and was not significantly different between PSP-cortical and PSP-subcortical groups (median 69 vs. 66 years). Patients with PSP-subcortical were younger at the time of death compared to those with PSP-cortical (the median (min, max) age at death was 73 (56, 82), and 78 (60, 87) respectively; p=0.01). The median (min, max) disease duration was 8 (3, 15) years with PSP-cortical participants having a longer disease duration at death (median 10 vs 6 years; p<0.001). The median (min, max) score for MoCA was 21 (0, 28) among all patients indicating possibly mild cognitive impairment, and it was not statistically significantly different between the PSP-cortical and PSP-subcortical groups (median 22 vs. 20). Comparison of groups revealed greater ocular motor abnormalities (based on the PSIS) in the PSP-Subcortical group (p= 0.003), while patients in the PSP-Cortical group had more severe apraxia of speech (p<0.001). There were no other significant differences between the two groups. Table 1. Patients’ demographics and clinical characteristics based on their last visit. Variables All patients PSP-cortical group PSP-subcortical group P-value Gender [f] 18 (40%) 8 (42%) 10 (38%) >0.99 APOE ε4 carrier 8 (26%) 4 (27%) 4 (25%) >0.99 Education [y] 16 (12, 20) 16 (12, 20) 16 (12, 20) 0.17 Age at onset [y] 67 (50, 78) 69 (54, 77) 66 (50, 78) 0.18 Disease duration [y] 5.7 (1.3, 10.8) 7.6 (3.1, 10.8) 4.2 (1.3, 9.8) <0.001 * Age at death [y] 75 (56, 87) 78 (60, 87) 73 (56, 82) 0.01 * Last clinical evaluation to death [y] 1.6 (1.0, 2.8) 2.5 (1.7, 3.0) 1.4 (0.9, 2.5) 0.07 Montreal Cognitive Assessment 21 (0, 28) 22 (0, 26) 20 (0, 28) 0.85 PSPRS 55 (5, 82) 54 (15, 82) 55 (5, 77) 0.51 PSPRS GM 16 (1, 20) 15 (1, 20) 16 (4, 20) 0.42 PSP Saccadic Impairment 4 (0, 5) 3 (0, 4) 4 (1, 5) 0.003 * Frontal Assessment Battery 12 (1, 18) 11 (1, 16) 13 (5, 18) 0.33 UPDRS III 62 (14, 92) 64 (15, 90) 62 (14, 92) >0.99 ASRS V3 9 (2, 49) 33 (16, 49) 8 (2, 11) <0.001 * Open in a new tab F= Female; Y= Years; Apo E= Apolipoprotein E; PSP = Progressive supranuclear palsy; PSPRS= Progressive Supranuclear Palsy- Rating Scale; PSPRS-G/M= Progressive Supranuclear Palsy - Rating Scale-gait/midlines; UPDRS= Movement Disorders Society- sponsored revision of the Unified Parkinson’s Disease Rating Scale; ASRS = Apraxia of Speech Rating Scale (version 3). * A p-value ≤0.05 was considered statistically significant. Tau lesion burden and distribution Bar charts of pathology scores for the four lesion types and their distribution in the 10 brain regions of interest are shown in esup Figure-1 . Analyzing the distribution pattern and burden of pre-tangles/neurofibrillary tangles revealed that in all 10 brain regions the majority of cases had moderate to severe tau lesion count. Regarding threads , in 8 of 10 regions more than 70% of patients had a moderate to severe burden; the exceptions were the superior frontal cortex and striatum. Coiled bodies were predominantly rated as mild in the substantia nigra and cerebellar dentate nucleus. In contrast, the globus pallidus, midbrain, precentral (motor) cortex, red nucleus, striatum, subthalamic nucleus, and ventral thalamus more commonly displayed a moderate to severe lesion count for coiled bodies. In 89% of the patients, there were no tufted astrocytes in the cerebellar dentate nucleus. The regions that most commonly showed moderate to severe burden of tufted astrocytes (>70% of cases) were the superior frontal cortex, midbrain, precentral (motor) cortex, and striatum. The relationship between regional tau burden and age at death Scatterplots of total tau burden against age at death can be found in Figure-1A . Based on the results from the Bayesian linear hierarchical regression models, younger age at death was associated with greater total tau burden in the globus pallidus (p<0.001), red nucleus (p=0.004), striatum (p=0.01), and subthalamic nucleus (p=0.01) with a five-year younger age associated with an estimated 0.5–0.7unit greater mean burden. Analyzing the PSP-cortical and PSP-subcortical groups separately, these associations remained in both groups for the specified regions (p<0.05 for all) with the exception of the subthalamic nucleus, which did not demonstrate a significant relationship with age at death in the PSP-Subcortical group (p=0.15) ( Table-2 ). Figure-1. Open in a new tab Scatter plots of summed pathology burden versus age at death and versus disease duration. The blue line is from an ordinary least squares regression line with 95% confidence intervals. This regression line indicates the mean burden by age and duration. Table-2. Estimates and 95% Bayesian credible intervals (CIs) summarizing the relationship between regional burden and age at death slope, and disease duration slope. Brain Regions All patients with PSP PSP-Cortical group PSP-Subcortical group Estimate (95% CI) P-value Estimate (95% CI) P-value Estimate (95% CI) P-value The relationship between regional burden and age at death slope Superior frontal cortex 0.2 (−0.2, 0.6) 0.40 −0.2 (−0.8, 0.3) 0.37 −0.1 (−0.5, 0.4) 0.78 Precentral (motor) cortex 0.1 (−0.3, 0.5) 0.54 −0.2 (−0.8, 0.3) 0.37 −0.2 (−0.6, 0.3) 0.50 Globus Pallidus −0.7 (−1.1, −0.3) <0.001 * −0.8 (−1.3, −0.3) 0.004 * −0.6 (−1.1, −0.1) 0.01 * Striatum −0.5 (−0.9, −0.1) 0.01 * −0.6 (−1.2, −0.1) 0.02 * −0.6 (−1.1, −0.1) 0.01 * Subthalamic nucleus −0.5 (−0.9, −0.1) 0.01 * −0.6 (−1.1, −0.1) 0.02 * −0.3 (−0.8, 0.1) 0.15 Ventral thalamus −0.3 (−0.7, 0.1) 0.15 −0.4 (−1.0, 0.1) 0.09 −0.3 (−0.7, 0.2) 0.24 Red nucleus −0.6 (−1.0, −0.2) 0.004 * −0.7 (−1.2, −0.2) 0.01 * −0.5 (−1.0, −0.1) 0.02 * Substantia nigra −0.3 (−0.7, 0.1) 0.16 −0.5 (−1.0, 0.1) 0.08 −0.2 (−0.7, 0.2) 0.27 Midbrain tegmentum −0.3 (−0.7, 0.1) 0.10 −0.4 (−0.9, 0.1) 0.10 −0.3 (−0.7, 0.2) 0.25 Cerebellar dentate nucleus 0.0 (−0.4, 0.4) 0.95 −0.2 (−0.7, 0.3) 0.49 0.0 (−0.4, 0.5) 0.97 The relationship between regional burden and disease duration slope Superior frontal cortex 0.9 (−0.3, 2.1) 0.15 0.0 (−1.4, 1.7) 0.96 −0.7 (−2.2, 0.8) 0.37 Precentral (motor) cortex 0.2 (−0.9, 1.4) 0.67 −0.5 (−2.0, 1.1) 0.53 −1.4 (−3.1, 0.1) 0.07 Globus Pallidus −0.6 (−1.8, 0.5) 0.27 −0.8 (−2.3, 0.6) 0.24 −0.5 (−1.9, 1.0) 0.51 Striatum 0.0 (−1.1, 1.1) 0.99 −0.3 (−1.6, 1.2) 0.69 −0.7 (−2.1, 0.8) 0.35 Subthalamic nucleus −1.0 (−2.1, 0.2) 0.09 −1.0 (−2.5, 0.3) 0.14 −0.7 (−2.2, 0.8) 0.36 Ventral thalamus −0.4 (−1.5, 0.7) 0.48 −0.6 (−1.9, 0.8) 0.41 −0.7 (−2.1, 0.8) 0.35 Red nucleus −1.2 (−2.4, 0.0) 0.05 * −1.2 (−2.7, 0.2) 0.10 −1.3 (−2.9, 0.1) 0.08 Substantia nigra −0.2 (−1.3, 0.9) 0.73 −0.6 (−2.0, 0.8) 0.37 −0.4 (−1.8, 1.2) 0.63 Midbrain tegmentum −0.7 (−1.8, 0.5) 0.24 −0.6 (−2.0, 0.9) 0.40 −0.8 (−2.3, 0.7) 0.28 Cerebellar dentate nucleus 0.3 (−0.9, 1.4) 0.63 −0.3 (−1.8, 1.2) 0.70 0.1 (−1.4, 1.8) 0.92 Open in a new tab * A p-value ≤0.05 was considered statistically significant. To explore the relationship between age at death and the burden of individual lesion types in these four regions among all patients, further analysis was conducted. This revealed similar associations between burden and age at death in all four lesion types, with no lesion type driving the overall associations ( Figure-2A ). Figure-2. Open in a new tab Scatter plot showing the relationship between summed pathology burden and burden for individual tau lesion types in specific brain regions by age at death and disease duration. The regression lines are from ordinary least squares and represent mean burden by at death and disease duration. The relationship between regional tau burden and disease duration Scatterplots of total tau burden against disease duration can be found in Figure-1B . Bayesian linear hierarchical regression models applied to all patients with PSP revealed that shorter disease duration was associated with greater total tau burden in the red nucleus with a five-year shorter duration be associated with a 1.2 unit higher burden (p=0.05) ( Table-2 ). When the analyses were conducted separately on patients with PSP-Cortical and PSP-Subcortical, no association was found between disease duration and tau burden in any of the 10 brain regions of interest, including the red nucleus (p>0.05 for all) although the estimates for the whole group and the two subgroups were very similar. No other regions showed significant associations between burden and disease duration. There was no evidence that this relationship between burden among all patients and disease duration differed across the four different tau lesion types in the red nucleus ( Figure-2B ). The relationship between co-pathologies and age at death and disease duration The odds of having ARTAG (OR=1.67 (1.10,2.73)) and AGD (OR=1.97 (1.16, 4.03)), were elevated among those who died at older ages for every 5 increases in age at death (p=0.02 and p=0.03, respectively); however, we did not observe significant association for disease duration. Regarding Alzheimer’s disease neuropathologic changes (ADNC), there was no clear association either for Thal>0 (any ADNC) compared to 0 (definite primary age-related tauopathy), or Braak 4–6 (higher ADNC) compared to Braak 1–3 (lower ADNC) with regards to age at death or disease duration (all p-values>0.05). Only three patients had a co-pathology of Lewy bodies; hence, further analysis was not performed for this co-pathology. Discussion In this study of forty-five well characterized patients with a definite diagnosis of PSP, we evaluated the relationship between tau burden in 10 brain regions and age at death and disease duration. We found that younger age at death was associated with greater total tau burden in the globus pallidus, striatum, red nucleus, and subthalamic nucleus, while shorter disease duration was associated with greater total tau burden in the red nucleus. We also found that associations were not driven by PSP subtype or by co-pathologies, especially AGD and ARTAG. The globus pallidus and subthalamic nucleus are among the most affected nuclei in PSP and atrophy of the subthalamic nucleus at autopsy is a biomarker for PSP diagnosis[ 19 ]. In our study, the highest burden of tau in the globus pallidus were for threads and coiled bodies, while in the subthalamic nucleus, pre tangles/globose tangles and threads were dominant. The globus pallidus plays a crucial role in the motor symptoms and regulation of voluntary movements in patients with PSP, both directly and indirectly with damage leading to disruption of the basal ganglia circuitry[ 20 ]. The globus pallidus is also involved in cognition, behavior, speech and swallowing through its connections with other brain regions (27, 28). The subthalamic nucleus is particularly affected in the early phase of PSP, with severity of its involvement being shown to be associated with the varius subtypes of PSP [ 21 ]. In the striatum (i.e., the caudate and putamen), tufted astrocytes were severe in most of the cases, while about a third of cases had severe burden for the other three lesions. The striatum and its connections play a fundamental role in motor and cognitive function in PSP [ 22 ]. Moreover, it has been reported that dopamine transporter bindings in the caudate significantly contribute to disease severity in PSP, and this relationship is independent of the impairment observed in motor, cognition and emotion [ 20 ]. Considering the high burden of tufted astrocytes in this brain region among our patients and that astrocytic degeneration does not contribute to gliosis or neuronal loss in PSP, it remains to be elucidated how tufted astrocytes relates to age at death and disease duration in these patients [ 23 ]. Regarding the red nucleus, which was the only region that showed a significant relationship with both age at death and disease duration, most of our patients had severe neuronal and oligodendroglial, but not astrocytic, tau accumulation in this region. The role of the red nucleus in PSP has not been extensively investigated. In one study apparent susceptibility, as measured by susceptibility weighted imaging, is increased in the red nucleus in PSP compared to patients with Parkinson’s disease, multiple system atrophy and healthy controls [ 24 ]. Moreover, in a recent study our group found that patients with PSP-RS and PSP-P exhibit increased susceptibility in the red nucleus, pallidum, substantia nigra, and cerebellar dentate compared to both patients with Parkinson’s disease and healthy controls. Furthermore, PSP-RS patients showed greater susceptibility than those with corticobasal syndrome. We found that susceptibility levels in the red nucleus varied between PSP subtypes as well, with the highest levels observed in the PSP-RS and PSP-P patients [ 25 ]. The role of the red nucleus in controlling swallowing has been demonstrated in animal studies; although its role in humans remains less understood [ 26 ]. Swallowing is a complex process involving both cortical and subcortical brain regions. Although the precise role of subcortical regions in swallowing mechanisms remains largely unexplored, it has been suggested that impairments in these areas are more strongly associated with a higher rate of dysphagia compared to dysfunctions in cortical regions [ 27 ]. Moreover, in a previous study our group found that impairment of the oral phase of swallowing was associated with reduced volume of the superior parietal regions, as well as abnormal diffusivity in parietal and sensorimotor white matter, superior longitudinal fasciculus, and posterior limb of the internal capsule. Meanwhile, impairment of the pharyngeal phase of swallowing was found to be associated with the medial frontal lobe, corticospinal tract and cerebral peduncle disruptions [ 28 ]. Considering that pneumonia, primarily resulting from silent aspiration due to swallowing dysfunction, is the most common cause of death in patients with PSP[ 29 ], it can be hypothesized that tau accumulation in subcortical regions, including the red nucleus, may lead to earlier impairment of swallowing dysfunction in PSP patients, thereby reducing their survival. Further studies are required to investigate the relationship between the neuroimaging characteristics of the red nucleus and disease duration. Such research might help in identifying a new biomarker for predicting survival in patients with PSP. In the comparison between the PSP-cortical and PSP-subcortical groups, a higher tau burden in the globus pallidus, striatum, and red nucleus was associated with a younger age at death for both groups. However, no relationship with disease duration was found in either group, although the group-wise estimates were similar to the estimate in the combined group. PSP variants display distinct patterns of involvement in the subcortical circuitry, as evidenced by MRI and flortaucipir PET imaging. [ 25 ]. In a previous study, we found that only the PSP-subcortical group, not the PSP-cortical group, exhibited cortical involvement and atrophy of the frontal cortex [ 21 ]. Interestingly, in the present study, we found no correlation between tau burden in the frontal regions and either age at death or disease duration, even in the PSP-subcortical group. These findings imply that while certain biomarkers are effective in diagnosing PSP and distinguishing between PSP-cortical and PSP-subcortical variants, they may not be reliable predictors of PSP prognosis. In terms of the specific tau lesions, in a previous study we found an inverse relationship between disease duration and glial tau burden in PSP [ 4 ]. A recent study on patients with PSP also reported that while neuronal tau burden was not statistically significantly different between those who survived for ≥10 years and those who died within the first 10 years of their symptom onset, coiled bodies were higher in the latter group. Moreover, a contrasting pattern in the density of tufted astrocytes compared to neuronal tau pathology and neurodegenerative changes has been previously reported [ 5 ]. Despite the previous reports, our further analysis aimed at identifying the specific lesion types contributing to the association with a younger age at death and shorter disease duration, found no evidence that any of coiled bodies, threads, tufted astrocytes, or neurofibrillary tangles is driving the association. This is in contrast to two previous studies that reported an inverse relationship between coiled bodies and disease duration[ 4 , 5 ]. Coiled bodies are Tau-positive cytoplasmic inclusions found in oligodendroglia. Contrasting with their counterparts in white matter, grey matter oligodendrocytes—referred to as perineuronal satellite oligodendrocytes—do not produce myelin. Despite this, they play vital roles in maintaining homeostasis and supporting neurons. Their function encompasses buffering potassium, bicarbonate, and chloride, as well as accumulating γ-amino butyric acid and producing neurotrophins ( 22 ). Dysfunction of oligodendrocytes has been recognized as playing a role in the pathogenesis of several neurodegenerative diseases, including multiple system atrophy, Alzheimer’s disease, and Parkinson’s disease ( 23 , 24 ). On the other hand, astrocytes and oligodendrocytes interact together to control local lipid metabolism of different brain regions and a dysregulation in their interactions is one of the pathogeneses of some of the neurological conditions ( 25 , 26 ). Despite that the specific sequence of tau accumulation among different cell types in PSP remains to be determined, in a study of PSP-RS, it has been suggested that the disease initiates with tau accumulation in neurons within the subcortical and brainstem nuclei. This is accompanied by early glial involvement in the globus pallidus and astroglial involvement in the striatum ( 3 ). It is not clear how these lesions all contribute similarly to the patients’ age at death and disease duration. Regarding co-pathologies, we found that patients with an older age at death were more likely to have ARTAG and AGD. ARTAG is a type of astroglial 4R tau pathology identified mainly in individuals over 60 years [ 12 ], similar to AGD another sporadic 4R tauopathy that has been found to be associated with cognitive impairment including slowly progressive amnesia and neuropsychiatric symptoms[ 30 ]. In our study, these co-pathologies did not show a significant relationship with disease duration. This aligns with one previous study reporting high prevalence of AGD in patients with PSP having no relationship with survival [ 5 ]. To our knowledge, no previous studies have assessed for relationships between age at death or disease duration and ARTAG, in PSP. One limitation of our study is the relatively limited sample size, which although is attributable to all patients being recruited from a single center and the rarity of PSP, is also a strength given that all had a standard clinical and neuropathological examination by two expert movement disorder specialist and one expert PSP neuropathologist. That is, all brain tissue from all patients were evaluated by a single neuropathologist, which enhances the accuracy and consistency of our findings. Due to the small sample sizes of the PSP-cortical and PSP-subcortical groups, we were unable to reliably analyze the burden of individual lesions on these groups separately. Another limitation is the lack of diversity in the cohort as most patients were Caucasians of European descent and hence, we do not know whether our results generalize to all races and ethnicities. We chose to analyze our data by summing individual lesion counts as opposed to taking the maximum of any lesion count within a specific region, to increase the range of the data and avoid a situation whereby all regions end up having a similar maximum score. Our analyses and results are based on tau burden but does account for neuronal loss which could also be playing a role. We chose not to account for co-pathologies in the main analyses due to our limited sample size and the fact that we are already assessing 10 regional associations. Our modeling approach protected us against over fitting. Adding co-pathologies may have led to unstable estimates. Finally, we acknowledge that may be a bias as to which patients came to autopsy. Conclusion: Our study indicates that younger age at death is associated with greater total tau burden in the globus pallidus, striatum, red nucleus, and subthalamic nucleus, while shorter disease duration is associated with greater total tau burden in the red nucleus. Moreover, the four cardinal lesions show a similar pattern with regards to age at death and disease duration in the brain regions. Further research is needed to investigate the mechanisms by which tau pathologies in these specific brain regions influence survival outcomes. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS2021851-supplement-1.tif (4.3MB, tif) 2 NIHMS2021851-supplement-2.docx (13.9KB, docx) Highlights: Younger age of death in progressive supranuclear palsy was related to greater tau burden in multiple subcortical nuclei. Shorter disease duration in progressive supranuclear palsy was associated with greater tau burden in the red nucleus. Relationships between tau burden, and age at death and disease duration do not appear to be driven by any one of the four cardinal pathologic lesions in progressive supranuclear palsy Acknowledgement We would like to thank the patients and their families for the brain donations to NRG that allowed us to conduct this study. Funding This study was funded by National Institutes of Health grants from the NINDS (R01- NS89757) and NIDCD (R01-DC12519 and R01- DC14942). Footnotes Financial disclosure and conflict of interest None. Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. Data availability statement The data related to this study is available upon reasonable request from the corresponding author. References: [1]. Litvan I, Agid Y, Calne D, Campbell G, Dubois B, Duvoisin RC, Goetz CG, Golbe LI, Grafman J, Growdon JH, Hallett M, Jankovic J, Quinn NP, Tolosa E, Zee DS, Clinical research criteria for the diagnosis of progressive supranuclear palsy (Steele-Richardson-Olszewski syndrome): report of the NINDS-SPSP international workshop, Neurology 47(1) (1996) 1–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed