INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6126,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12340429','Plasma tau biomarkers are distinctly associated with tau tangles and decreased with Lewy body pathology.','Montoliu-Gaya L, Valeriano-Lorenzo E, Ashton NJ, López-González FJ, Lantero-Rodriguez J, Ruiz-González A, Pastor AB, Frades B, Zea-Sevilla MA, Valentí M, Ruiz-Valderrey P, Saiz L, Burgueño-García I, López-Martínez MJ, Del Ser T, Brinkmalm G, Zetterberg H, Rábano A, Gobom J, Blennow K, Sánchez-Juan P.','2025','vallecas reina tau205 tau205 tau205 tau199 tau231 tau231 tau205 tau231 adncs coexistent tau199 tau202 tau205 tau231 tau195 tau212 vallecas reina vars vars vars biannual neary smmse tau205 tau199 vars mcb rmsfc expte cien vars vacutainer k2edta thawed vortexed perchloric desalting aqua lyophilized reconstituted trifluoroacetic bisected hemibrain hemibrain parasagittal eclipse 90i apochromatic m087201 mn1060 ab7254 ncl proteintech lpc deramecourt vcing uncus juxtacortical profiler 20x tfptau tau205 ppcor aroc s1b survminer inflating vars tau199 tau199 tau205 tau205 fmol fmol fmol fmol tau199 fmol tau202 fmol tau205 fmol tau231 fmol tau205 tau199 tau231 tau205 tau205 tau231 tau217ratio tau205 tau205ratio tau231 tau217ratio tau205 tau205ratio tau205 tau199 tau231 tau205 tau199 percentual tau205 tau205 tau205 tau205 tau199 tau202 tau205 tau231 s3a tau205 tau205 tau231 tau205 tau205 tau199 tau199 s3b tau205 ρ195 ρ212 tau199 tau205 s1b lpc tau195 tau199 tau202 tau205 tau231 tau199 tau205 tau199 lpc vi6 lpc stablished lpc lpc lpc lpc tau199 tau231 tau205 lpc lpc lpc lpc lpc lpc lpc lpc lpc3 lpc lpc lpc tau205 tau199 signif signif tau199 tau195 tau199 tau202 tau195 tau199 dichotomization tau199 tau195 tau202 misinterpreting tau205 tau199 tau205 tau205 tau205 tau205 tau205 tau199 tau199 deramecourt lpc elevates compensates tau205 tau199 compensating tau199 tau199 tau195 tau199 tau199 henrik acumen alector alzinova alzpath amylyx annexon apellis aztherapies cognito cogrx denali labcorp merry nervgen optoceutics pinteon prothena quanterix abbey remynd samumed alzecure bioarctic cellectricon fujirebio webmd gothenburg ventures kaj bioarctic jomdd shimadzu magqu pharmatrophix prothena gothenburg ventures e70562 e70562 vallecas reina vars reina laia montoliu gaya brightfocus a2022015f bertil stohnes ahlen stifelsen alzheimerfonden gamla tjanarinnor kaj addf rdapb hjarnfonden fo2017 alf alfgbg jpnd2019 1r01ag068398 zenith zen henrik wallenberg alfgbg addf adsf adsf adsf adsf metrology neurobiostand 22hlt07 bluefield olav thon erling familjen ronstroms stiftelse stiftelsen gamla tjanarinnor hjarnfonden fo2022 skłodowska miriade jpnd2021 ukdri vars isciii pmp22 pi23 ted2021 131676b cien montoliu gaya valeriano e70562 laia montoliu gaya valeriano kaj dage karikari brinkmalm edelmayer lantero karikari calvet palmqvist quiroz carlgren e14022 moloney kouri pascoal karikari tau231 incipient salvado e17123 montoliu gaya benedet tissot spectrometric montoliu gaya alosco yhang hye rajkumar karikari brinkmalm olazaran aguera osorio reina marson parrish neuropsychologic reina burgueno uceda heras vallecas reina gobom benedet carlgren uceda heras zea sevilla rabano steur deramecourt slade skrobot esiri vcing merrick longev e115 e125 barthelemy dage bali brum molfetta barthelemy mathurin dominantly barthelemy saef occupancies t217 t205 lantero montoliu gaya benedet tau205 immunocytochemistry collij mastenbroek carlgren tosun hausle thropp tosun yardibi benzinger londos galvan przybelski algeciras schimnich tau231 bolsewig unnik blujdea e209418 tosun hausle iwaki khandelwal hypometabolic hachinski merskey colloby mcaleese steenoven koel simmelink mja vergouw ljm lantero tissot snellman e70562 e70562','Abstract INTRODUCTION Plasma‐to‐autopsy studies are essential to understand how tau blood biomarkers change in relation to Alzheimer''s disease (AD) brain pathology and how they are influenced by brain co‐pathologies and comorbidities. METHODS Plasma samples from 102 brain donors of the Vallecas Alzheimer Reina Sofia cohort were analyzed using a mass spectrometry method to measure the levels of six phosphorylated and two non‐phosphorylated tau biomarkers. RESULTS In cases with high pathological burden of AD, phosphorylated tau (p‐tau)217 showed associations with neurofibrillary tangle counts across all regions, while p‐tau205 was primarily linked to the frontal cortex, and the non‐phosphorylated tau peptides were linked to the temporal cortex. Plasma tau levels decreased as Lewy body pathology progressed, even among individuals at the same tau Braak stage. All plasma biomarkers correlated with creatinine levels, as a marker of renal dysfunction, but this effect was mitigated when using the ratios phospho/non‐phospho. DISCUSSION Understanding how plasma tau biomarkers are influenced by co‐pathologies and comorbidities is crucial for their accurate implementation. Highlights The plasma phosphorylated tau (p‐tau)217 ratio, followed by the p‐tau205 ratio and p‐tau217, were the best‐performing biomarkers for detecting neuropathologically confirmed Alzheimer''s disease (AD). In high AD cases, p‐tau217 showed associations with neurofibrillary tangle (NFT) counts across all regions, while p‐tau205 was primarily linked to the frontal cortex, and non‐phosphorylated tau peptides were linked to the temporal cortex. We observed a decline in plasma tau levels as Lewy body pathology progressed, even among individuals at the same tau Braak stage. All plasma biomarkers correlated with creatinine levels, as a marker of renal dysfunction, but this effect was mitigated when using the ratios. Plasma p‐tau199 displayed a distinct behavior compared to other p‐tau species, showing no association with NFT counts in any brain region, but demonstrating significant correlations with brain volume and an effect on survival time. Keywords: blood, mass spectrometry, neuropathological examination, tau phosphorylation 1. BACKGROUND Approximately 25% to 30% of patients diagnosed with Alzheimer''s disease (AD) in specialized dementia clinics are misdiagnosed when biomarkers are not used. 1 The rate of misdiagnosis is even higher in primary care, where most individuals with cognitive or behavioral symptoms are first assessed. 2 Blood‐based biomarkers, particularly phosphorylated tau (p‐tau), have the potential to greatly enhance diagnostic accuracy by offering accessible, efficient, cost‐effective, and reliable diagnostic tools. 3 Integrating high‐performance plasma p‐tau assays into clinical practice could decrease the dependence on more costly procedures like cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis or positron emission tomography (PET). 4 This improvement is especially timely given the recent advancements in anti‐amyloid beta (Aβ) immunotherapies. Although CSF and PET biomarkers acquired at the same time of plasma sampling can be used to validate blood biomarkers, 5 neuropathological examination of Aβ plaques and tau neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs) not only serves to confirm the diagnostic accuracy of blood biomarkers but also precisely assesses their correlation with specific brain pathologies. 6 In 2020, blood p‐tau181 became the first blood p‐tau biomarker to be validated against neuropathology, 7 demonstrating improved accuracy in AD diagnosis compared to clinical diagnosis alone. Subsequent studies further validated other p‐tau biomarkers, such as p‐tau217 8 , 9 , 10 and p‐tau231 11 , 12 against neuropathological examination. More recent works have investigated the independent associations of plasma p‐tau markers with AD neuropathological hallmarks. One study using immunoassays found that p‐tau231 plasma levels were specifically associated with amyloid plaques, whereas p‐tau181 and p‐tau217 were independently associated with both plaques and tangles, 13 corroborating previous findings using PET imaging biomarkers. 14 Another study used mass spectrometry (MS) to quantify several plasma tau markers, revealing that concentrations of both p‐tau and non‐phosphorylated tau peptides were elevated in neuropathologically confirmed AD cases. 15 Among these, p‐tau217, p‐tau205, and p‐tau231 exhibited the largest fold‐changes in AD compared to controls. Furthermore, the evaluation of tau peptide levels in relation to Consortium to Establish a Registry for Alzheimer''s Disease (CERAD) and Braak classifications indicated that these phosphorylated tau species increase at different stages of amyloid and tau pathology (CERAD and Braak, respectively). 15 Another critical aspect that neuropathological studies can investigate is the influence of brain co‐pathologies on blood biomarker levels. AD neuropathological changes (ADNCs) are frequently associated with a high prevalence of coexistent Lewy body disease (LBD), TAR DNA‐binding protein 43 (TDP‐43), and/or other proteinopathies, which influence the clinical phenotype. 16 , 17 Unfortunately, the availability of imaging and fluid biomarkers to detect these additional pathologies remains limited or, in some cases, non‐existent. 18 Consequently, determining in vivo whether brain co‐pathologies coexist with AD remains challenging. In this context, neuropathological examination continues to be the most reliable method for identifying the presence of multiple pathologies. Although p‐tau is recognized as a specific biomarker for AD, 19 the influence of other brain pathologies—such as Lewy bodies and cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA)—on blood tau levels has not been extensively investigated. Gaining a better understanding of these interactions is crucial for accurately interpreting blood tau biomarkers in clinical practice. Here, we used a validated MS method to examine the association between six p‐tau (p‐tau181, p‐tau199, p‐tau202, p‐tau205, p‐tau217, p‐tau231) and two non‐phosphorylated (tau195‐205, tau212‐221) tau species in ante mortem plasma samples from 102 brain donors from the Vallecas Alzheimer Reina Sofia (VARS) cohort. This cohort is notable not only for its comprehensive neuropathological brain assessments but also for the minimal interval between plasma extraction and death, enhancing the accuracy of correlations between blood biomarkers and neuropathological findings. We investigated the ability of various plasma tau species to detect AD brain pathology, both independently and in the context of other co‐pathologies. Additionally, we explored their relationships with NFT counts and multiple neuropathological scales across different brain regions. Furthermore, we evaluated the impact of brain co‐pathologies and renal dysfunction on plasma tau levels, examined their association with brain atrophy, and identified which tau species best predicted survival time. 2. METHODS 2.1. Study design This study was based on post mortem brain donations from the VARS study. The participants and their families decide to specifically participate in the clinical, biochemical, genetic, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and neuropathological (i.e., brain donation) programs. Routine clinical evaluation of patients in the VARS cohort includes baseline and biannual assessment of several cognitive, functional, and behavioral traits. Sex, age at first symptom onset according to medical records and caretaker interviews, global deterioration stage, clinical diagnosis according to standard diagnostic criteria (McKhann for probable and possible AD, McKeith for dementia with Lewy bodies, Neary for frontotemporal dementia, and the evidence of relevant vascular lesions in brain MRI for mixed dementia), 20 and severe Mini‐Mental State Examination (sMMSE) 21 paired with plasma tau determination at the ante mortem evaluation, the closest to death date, were obtained. RESEARCH IN CONTEXT Systematic review : We performed a literature review using PubMed and meeting abstracts and presentations. While blood tau biomarkers have been widely studied, few publications have investigated the effects of co‐pathologies and comorbidities, and even fewer in a neuropathologically confirmed cohort. Interpretation : In this article, we show for the first time that tau plasma biomarkers (i.e., phosphorylated tau (p‐tau217, p‐tau205, p‐tau199, and non‐phosphorylated tau) are differently associated with neurofibrillary tangles in several brain regions. In addition, we show that the levels of these biomarkers are affected by the presence of brain co‐pathologies like Lewy body dementia, and peripheral comorbidities like renal dysfunction. Although the latter has been previously described, this is the first time shown in a neuropathological cohort. Finally, we show that these biomarkers also have different associations with time to death. Future directions : Future studies should address how to consider these effects to establish accurate cut‐offs for adequately implementing tau blood‐based biomarkers. We included all individuals whose brains had already been collected and provided ante mortem blood samples for our analyses. The characteristics of the patients and cohort have been described elsewhere. 22 , 23 2.2. Ethics approval Local health authorities have approved the VARS patient follow‐up program at the Queen Sofía Alzheimer''s Centre (Ref: MCB/RMSFC, Expte: 12672). The CIEN Tissue Bank is a biobank authorized by local and national health authorities (National Registry number 0000741). 2.3. Blood collection All participants from the VARS were required to fast for at least 2 hours before the blood sampling. Venous blood was drawn and collected into plasma tubes (Vacutainer K2EDTA 13 × 100 mm, 6 mL). Within 2 hours of collection, samples were centrifuged twice, first for 10 minutes at 1700 × g at 4°C, then plasma was transferred into another tube and centrifuged for 15 minutes at 2500 x g at 4°C and stored at −80°C until their use. 2.4. Plasma tau biomarker quantification by MS Plasma samples were analyzed with a previously described MS method. 14 Briefly, 1 mL EDTA plasma was thawed, vortexed at 2000 rpm for 30 seconds, and then centrifuged at 4000 × g for 10 minutes. Tau protein was extracted through immunoprecipitation (IP) and perchloric acid precipitation, followed by solid phase desalting. Heavy‐labeled AQUA peptide standards were spiked in during the sample preparation. The samples underwent digestion with trypsin for 18 hours, after which they were lyophilized and stored at –80°C. Prior to liquid chromatography (LC)‐MS analysis, the samples were reconstituted in 50 µL of 0.05% trifluoroacetic acid and analyzed in single runs. The LC settings matched those detailed in prior work. 24 Plasma sample analysis was conducted without access to any participant information to ensure blinding. 2.5. Brain processing Main neuropathological procedures for brain extraction and immediate processing have been previously described. 25 Shortly after extraction, the brain was weighed and bisected through a mid‐sagittal section. The right half hemibrain was fast‐frozen, while the left hemibrain was fixed by immersion in 4% buffered formaldehyde for complete neuropathological processing. After 3 weeks of fixation, the left cerebral hemisphere was cut in serial coronal slices, the cerebellum in parasagittal slices, and the brainstem in transversal slices. Multiple tissue blocks were dissected from cortical and subcortical regions for paraffin embedding, and 4 µm paraffin sections were obtained for routine hematoxylin/eosin (H/E) examination and immunohistochemistry. All histological assessments of this study were performed under a Nikon Eclipse 90i optical microscope using white light and apochromatic optics. 2.6. Neuropathological evaluation Neuropathological classification of all cases was performed according to published consensus criteria. For that purpose, a panel of immunohistochemical stains was applied to selected brain regions based on the following primary antibodies: Aβ protein (Dako, code: M087201‐2), tau AT100 (ThermoFisher Invitrogen, code: MN1060), ubiquitin (Abcam, code: ab7254), α‐synuclein (α‐syn; Leica Biosystems, code: 1 mL NCL‐L‐ASYN), and TDP‐43 (Proteintech, code: 10782‐2‐AP), according to the manufacturer''s instructions. For the assessment of ADNC (either null, low, intermediate, or high probability), we used the ABC method recommended by the National Institute on Aging (NIA), including the evaluation of Thal amyloid stage (0–5), NIA‐A stage (0–3), Braak tau stage (0–VI), NIA‐B stage (0–3), and CERAD NIA‐C stage (0–3). 26 Lewy bodies (LBs) were assessed through Braak α‐syn staging (0–6) 27 and LB pathology consensus (LPC) staging (0–5). 28 For the assessment of cerebrovascular lesions associated with dementia, we used the total vascular score (TVS) defined by Deramecourt et al. 29 (0–20) that includes partial assessment of vascular pathology in the frontal and temporal lobes (0–6), basal ganglia (0–4), and hippocampus (0–4). Vascular Cognitive Impairment Neuropathology Guidelines (VCING) criteria for assessing vascular pathology burden in dementia patients were also applied. 30 Additionally, TDP‐43 proteinopathy was evaluated according to the definition and staging of limbic‐predominant age‐related TDP‐43 encephalopathy (LATE; 0–3). 31 The stage of hippocampal sclerosis (HS; 0–4), as defined recently by our group, 25 was performed at two coronal levels of the hippocampus, separated by the tip of the uncus: head (anterior HS) and body (posterior HS). Other frequently combined pathologies assessed were argyrophilic grain disease (Saito stages, 0–3) and aging‐related tau astrogliopathy. 32 The latter was registered as present or absent, both in the medial temporal lobe and the cerebral neocortex/juxtacortical white matter. 2.7. Quantification of Aβ area and NFT number The Aβ total area and the number of NFTs were measured by digital analysis with the Cell Profiler program on photomicrographs of 20x fields. The measures were assessed in sections of paraffin stained with AT100 for tau and 6F/3D for Aβ in CERAD areas: lateral temporal cortex (BA 22), dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (BA 8‐9) and inferior parietal cortex (BA 39‐40). The associations between each tau species in plasma and the two neuropathological measures of AD pathology, amyloid burden and number of NFTs, were assessed independently. Both Aβ total area and the number of NFTs are continuous measures; the former represents the percentage of Aβ total area (Aβ area) stained in post mortem tissue, and the latter represents the amount of tangle accumulation in three different cortical regions (temporal, frontal, and parietal). 2.8. Statistical analyses Data analyses and visualization were performed using R version 4.2.1. Significance was determined at p < 0.05 (two tailed). Plasma tau concentrations were tested for normality using visual inspection based on normal Q‐Q plot of the values, the first and third quartiles. The distribution of plasma tau variants was skewed (positive skew), so the values were transformed by their square root transformation (tfptau) when the assumptions for the statistical method required normal distribution. The ratios’ values were calculated with raw scores of plasma tau, values considered outliers for the ratio of p‐tau217 (one subject above 3 standard deviation [SD]), ratio of p‐tau205 (one subject above 3 SD) were excluded from the analysis. Differences in demographics and clinical characteristics across the groups were assessed with the chi‐squared statistic for categorical variables or the Wilcoxon test for continuous variables. First, the Kruskal–Wallis rank sum test and unpaired two‐sample Wilcoxon test were conducted to examine the differences in plasma tau concentrations between the neuropathological groups (AD, AD+, and non‐AD) and between clinical diagnoses. Additionally, partial Spearman correlation, ppcor (version 1.1) package, was performed to assess the strength of the association of plasma tau concentrations with neuropathology and cognition while controlling for the effect of the age at extraction, time elapsed between collection and death date (days), estimated age at onset, and sex. Second, the diagnostic accuracy of plasma tau concentrations was assessed using receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves, with sex, apolipoprotein E ε4, and age at plasma extraction as covariates. The AROC (version 1.0‐4) package was used to analyze ROC curves adjusted by covariates. The DeLong test was conducted to compare two correlated ROC curves analyzing the differences in their respective areas under the curve (AUC) using the pROC (version 1.18‐0) package. Third, independent multiple regression analyses were conducted to test the effect of brain co‐pathologies, those were dichotomized following the approach of Nichols et al. 33 as independent variables (Table S1B in supporting information). Adjustment by relevant covariates (age at extraction, age at onset, number of days elapsed between extraction and death, and sex) was applied and they were centralized before conducting the analyses. Fourth, the associations between each tau species in plasma and the two neuropathological measures of AD pathology, amyloid total area and number of NFTs, were assessed independently. Partial Spearman ρ tested the tau variants’ association with the total amount of pathology in each cortical area and the sum of those three areas. Adjustments by age, sex, age at onset, and amyloid pathology (when the number of NFTs was the main variable) and tau pathology (when the amyloid area was the main variable) were performed. Fifth, the effect of plasma tau variants on the patients’ survival time was assessed using a multiple linear regression model. Then, the survival rates related to plasma tau species with significant effects in the previous analysis were also tested and graphically represented using the survival (version 3.5‐3) and survminer (version 0.4‐9) packages in R. The study was both hypothesis driven and exploratory, aiming to detect meaningful associations between plasma tau species and neuropathological features. Formal correction for multiple comparisons was not applied due to the small sample size and the risk of inflating type II errors. All statistical results, including exact p values and confidence intervals, are reported in the corresponding tables. 3. RESULTS 3.1. Sample characteristics and plasma tau biomarkers Plasma samples and clinical and neuropathological data were obtained from 102 brain donors from the VARS cohort. The average time from blood draw to death was 142.9 ± 144.2 days. The patients had been diagnosed upon admission to the nursing home: 69 cases with AD dementia, 29 with mixed AD, and 3 non‐AD cases (Table S1 ). Brain donors were grouped according to neuropathological examination in AD (high amount of AD pathology with intermediate or high ADNC scores), AD+ (AD pathology with intermediate or high ADNC scores and other associated pathologies, such as LB or vascular lesions), and non‐AD (high amount of other pathology and low ADNC score). No significant differences were observed regarding age at extraction, time from blood draw to death, estimated age at onset, and age at death among these three groups. The average plasma concentrations of six phosphorylated (p‐tau181, 199, 202, 205, 217, and 231), and two non‐phosphorylated (195‐209, 212‐221) tau peptides, together with the ratio p‐tau217/212‐221 (p‐tau217 ratio), p‐tau199/195‐209 (p‐tau199 ratio), and p‐tau205/195‐209 (p‐tau205 ratio) are shown in Table 1 . The correlations between plasma tau biomarkers are presented in Figure S1 in supporting information. TABLE 1. Sample characteristics. Neuropathological groups Total sample ( n = 102) a AD group ( n = 25) AD + group ( n = 59) Non‐AD group ( n = 18) Variable Mean (SD) Median [range] Mean (SD) Median [range] Mean (SD) Median [IQR] [range] Mean (SD) Median [IQR] [range] Age at extraction 87.7 (6.1) 88.3 [66.4–104.5] 88.982 (4.893) 88.9 [79.8–97.4] 87.0 (6.2) 87.550 [66.404–101.50] 88.3 (7.1) 88.3 [75.6–104.5] Time from 