''s disease; p‐tau, phosphorylated tau. Distribution of p‐tau181 according to the clinical diagnosis (A) or to the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (B). Distribution of p‐tau217 according to the clinical diagnosis (C) or to the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (D). Distribution of p‐tau205 according to the clinical diagnosis (E) or to the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (F). Distribution of p‐tau199 according to the clinical diagnosis (G) or the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (H). Distribution of p‐tau231 according to the clinical diagnosis (I) or to the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (J). Distribution of the p‐tau217 ratio according to the clinical diagnosis (K) or to the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (L). Distribution of the p‐tau205 ratio according to the clinical diagnosis (M) or to the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (N). Distribution of the p‐tau199 ratio according to the clinical diagnosis (O) or to the post‐mortem neuropathological assessment (P). FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Specific association between tau species in plasma to tau pathology assessed in post mortem tissue. A complete sample was analyzed using partial Spearman ρ correlation to test the association between each tau variant in plasma and the post mortem number of neurofibrillary tangles in three cortical regions, plus the sum of the three areas. The ρ coefficients were estimated after adjustment by amyloid total area and for covariates (age at plasma collection, sex, age at onset). The percentual values (%) correspond to the percentage of the specific partial Spearman ρ;, it has been calculated with the formula: 100*[partial ρ / (partial ρ NFTs + partial ρ Aβ )]. + p < 0.10; * p < 0.05; ** p < 0.01; *** p < 0.001. p‐tau, phosphorylated tau. Next, we assessed the discriminatory ability of each biomarker to distinguish between neuropathologically confirmed AD, AD+, and non‐AD cases (Table 2 ). The plasma p‐tau217 ratio emerged as the biomarker with the highest discriminative power to differentiate AD from non‐AD (AUC = 0.97 [95% confidence interval (CI) 0.90–1]), AD+ from non‐AD (AUC = 0.95 [95% CI 0.86–1]), and combined AD&AD+ from non‐AD cases (AUC = 0.92 [95% CI 0.88–0.99]). The p‐tau205 ratio showed the second highest discriminative power: AUC AD vs. non‐AD = 0.85 [95% CI 0.62–0.94], AUC AD+ vs. non‐AD = 0.87 [95% CI 0.76–0.95], AUC AD&AD+ vs. non‐AD = 0.87 [95% CI 0.79–0.94]); and p‐tau217, the third: AUC AD vs. non‐AD = 0.88 [95% CI 0.66–0.99], AUC AD+ vs. non‐AD = 0.83 [95% CI 0.74–0.95], AUC AD&AD+ vs. non‐AD = 0.84 [95% CI 0.72–0.92]). The accuracy values of p‐tau217 ratio were not statistically different from those of p‐tau205 ratio to discriminate AD+ from non‐AD, but they were statistically higher compared to p‐tau217 and p‐tau205 ratio to discriminate AD from non‐AD cases (Table S2 in supporting information). TABLE 2. The discriminative power of tau species to classify AD, AD+, and non‐AD cases. Adjusted model a Ante mortem peptide concentration AD&AD+ versus non‐AD AD versus non‐AD AD versus AD+ AD+ versus non‐AD AUC [95% CI] AUC [95% CI] AUC [95% CI] AUC [95% CI] p‐tau217 ratio 0.92 [0.88 – 0.99] 0.97 [0.9 – 1] 0.51 [0.33–0.65] 0.95 [0.86–1] p‐tau205 ratio 0.87 [0.79 – 0.94] 0.85 [0.62 – 0.94] 0.46 [0.31–0.61] 0.87 [0.76–0.95] p‐tau217 0.84 [0.72 – 0.92] 0.88 [0.66 – 0.99] 0.57 [0.39–0.77] 0.83 [0.74–0.95] p‐tau181 0.68 [0.57–0.74] 0.69 [0.54–0.82] 0.59 [0.4–0.75] 0.64 [0.52–0.75] Tau 212‐221 0.59 [0.47–0.75] 0.6 [0.36–0.77] 0.55 [0.42–0.69] 0.5 [0.31–0.68] Tau 195‐209 0.66 [0.51–0.81] 0.68 [0.52–0.88] 0.54 [0.4–0.76] 0.56 [0.37–0.75] p‐tau199 0.60 [0.46–0.82] 0.7 [0.53–0.83] 0.62 [0.45–0.77] 0.55 [0.39–0.75] p‐tau202 0.57 [0.38–0.73] 0.58 [0.37–0.8] 0.61 [0.43–0.74] 0.53 [0.27–0.77] p‐tau205 0.76 [0.58–0.92] 0.75 [0.58–0.89] 0.52 [0.42–0.62] 0.71 [0.57–0.86] p‐tau231 0.678 [0.504–0.858] 0.74 [0.6–0.92] 0.59 [0.45–0.73] 0.66 [0.53–0.81] Open in a new tab Notes : AUC values > 0.80 and no significant difference of two unpaired ROC curves are in bold, AUC p‐tau217 ratio as the reference group. AD group involves patients with predominant AD, the AD+ group involves cases classified as AD (+LBD), AD(+VaD), and AD (+VaD +LBD), and the non‐AD group implies VaD, VaD(+LBD), non (AD, VaD, LBD), and LBD cases. a Model adjusted for APOE ε4 (non‐ APOE ε4 is the reference group), age at plasma collection (centered in the median 88,4), and sex (female reference group). Abbreviations: AD, Alzheimer''s disease; APOE , apolipoprotein E; AUC, area under the curve; CI, confidence interval; LBD, Lewy body dementia; p‐tau, phosphorylated tau; ROC, receiver operating characteristic; VaD, vascular dementia. 3.3. Plasma tau species in relation to tangle count and ABC score We then investigated the association between each plasma tau species and NFT count in different brain regions: temporal cortex, frontal cortex, and parietal cortex (Figure 2 , and Figure S3 and Table S3A in supporting information). A positive association between the number of NFTs and the concentrations of most plasma tau species was found in temporal and frontal cortex of the whole sample: p‐tau217 (temporal: ρ = 0.47, p < 0.001; frontal: ρ = 0.38, p < 0.001), p‐tau205 (temporal: ρ = 0.35, p < 0.001; frontal: ρ = 0.41, p < 0.001), p‐tau217 ratio (temporal: ρ = 0.40, p < 0.001; frontal: ρ = 0.35, p < 0.001), and p‐tau205 ratio (temporal: ρ = 0.21, p = 0.045; frontal: ρ = 0.40. p < 0.001), p‐tau181 (temporal: ρ = 0.27, p = 0.010; frontal: ρ = 0.25, p = 0.016), p‐tau231 (temporal: ρ = 0.26, p = 0.013; frontal: ρ = 0.22, p = 0.039), and two non‐phosphorylated tau peptides, 195‐209 (temporal: ρ = 0.28, p = 0.007; frontal: ρ = 0.22, p = 0.035), and 212‐221 (temporal: ρ = 0.29, p = 0.006; Frontal: ρ = 0.25, p = 0.019). In the parietal cortex, only p‐tau217 (ρ = 0.38, p < 0.001), p‐tau205 (ρ = 0.25, p < 0.001), p‐tau217 ratio (ρ = 0.36, p < 0.001), and p‐tau205 ratio (ρ = 0.22, p = 0.039) showed significant associations. Interestingly, plasma p‐tau199 did not correlate with NFTs in any brain region and the p‐tau199 ratio showed a negative and significant association (ρ = –0.22, p = 0.039) in the temporal area. A sensitivity analysis conducted only in cases with high ADNC score (Table S3B ) showed that p‐tau217 was the only variant with a significant relationship with the number of NFTs, independently of Aβ area, in all quantified regions (temporal: ρ = 0.33, p = 0.005; frontal: ρ = 0.24, p = 0.043; parietal: ρ = 0.26, p = 0.024). Plasma p‐tau205 evidenced a significant association only in the frontal region (ρ = 0.28, p = 0.016), and the two non‐phosphorylated (195‐209, 212‐221) tau peptides showed significant correlations only in the temporal area (ρ195‐209 = 0.25, p = 0.031; ρ212‐221 = 0.26, p = 0.028). Plasma p‐tau199 ratio had a negative and significant relationship in both temporal (ρ = –0.24, p = 0.044) and parietal (ρ = –0.29, p = 0.013) regions. Finally, a complementary approach was developed to test the association between tau concentrations in plasma and the score of neuropathological scales based on ABC Score (NIA A guidelines; Table S4 in supporting information). Partial Kendall τ was conducted with adjustment for age, sex, and age at onset. Similar results were obtained, showing a significant association between plasma concentrations of most tau species and the neuropathology stages in independent models for the complete sample after adjustment for covariates. Interestingly, p‐tau217, p‐tau205, and the respective ratios, were the most specific markers for tau load (NIA B), even when adjusting tau by amyloid and vice versa. 3.4. Effect of mixed pathologic burden on plasma tau species levels We tested the effect of nine different neuropathological scales (Table S1B , Figure S4 in supporting information) on plasma tau species. Independent linear models were built with every pathological staging as the predictor. Additionally, to assess the effect of each neuropathological factor independently of AD pathology, we included Braak staging in all the models (Braak ≤ IV coded as 0 and Braak V and VI coded as 1). We found that Lewy body pathology consensus criteria score (LPC) showed a negative and significant association with non‐phosphorylated tau195‐209 (β = –0.37; p = 0.036), and the six phosphorylated tau species: p‐tau217 (β = –0.01; p = 0.048), p‐tau181 (β = –0.03; p = 0.013), p‐tau199 (β = –0.01; p = 0.002), p‐tau202 (β = –0.004; p = 0.021), p‐tau205 (β = –2e‐04; p = 0.014), p‐tau231(β = –0.04; p = 0.020), and p‐tau199 (β = –0.01; p = 0.009; Table S5 in supporting information). None of the ratios—p‐tau217 ratio, p‐tau205 ratio, and p‐tau199 ratio—showed any correlation with LPC scores. A deeper analysis was conducted to examine the effect of combined LB and AD pathologies. For that purpose, patients with Braak stages V and VI6 (ADNC = 3) were selected to control for a similar staging of AD pathology in them. Then, we disaggregated the ordinal score of LPC by the localization of α‐syn deposits and three groups were stablished: 1 AD‐Asyn group: cases with AD plus non‐α‐syn deposits (LPC = 0) or deposits in olfactory bulb (LPC = 1), 2 AD+Asyn‐Amy: cases with AD plus α‐syn deposits in the amygdala (LPC = 2), and 3 AD+Asyn‐Lim/Neo cases with AD plus α‐syn deposits in limbic and neocortical regions (LPC = 4&5). The concentrations of p‐tau were significantly lower in AD+Asyn‐Lim/Neo group compared to AD+Asyn‐amy and AD‐Asyn cases: p‐tau181 (1 vs. 3, p = 0.014; 2 vs. 3, p = 0.024), p‐tau199 (1 vs. 3, p = 0.043; 2 vs. 3, p = 0.001), p‐tau217 (1 vs. 3, p = 0.032; 2 vs. 3, p = 0.019), and p‐tau231 (1 vs. 3, p = 0.023; 2 vs. 3, p = 0.013). Furthermore, the p‐tau205 (2 vs. 3, p = 0.020) and the ratio of p‐tau217 (1 vs. 3, p = 0.027) evidenced lower concentrations in the AD+Asyn‐Lim/Neo only compared to AD+Asyn‐amy and AD predominant cases (AD‐Asyn), respectively (Figure 3 ). The rest of the tau variants did not show a statistically significant difference between groups (Figure S5 in supporting information). FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Tau variants distribution according to combined AD and Lewy body pathologies. Mixed pathology groups were established based on Braak stages and Lewy pathology consensus criteria (LPC score); AD‐Asyn group was composed of cases with AD plus non‐α‐synuclein deposits (LPC = 0) or deposits in the olfactory bulb (LPC = 1); AD+Asyn‐Amy group comprised of cases with AD plus α‐synuclein deposits in the amygdala (LPC = 2); AD+Asyn‐Lim/Neo involving cases with AD plus α‐synuclein deposits in limbic and neocortical regions (LPC = 4&5). LPC 0: no deposition ( n = 45. 45.9%); LPC 1: deposition in the olfactory bulb ( n = 11. 11.2%); LPC 2: deposition in the amygdala ( n = 18. 18.4%); LPC3: deposition in substantia nigra ( n = 1. 1.0%); LPC 4:. deposition in the cingulate cortex ( n = 10. 10.2%); LPC 5: deposition in the frontal cortex ( n = 13. 13.3%). Due to our sample only including one subject in the LPC = 3 score, it was excluded from the analysis. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; p‐tau, phosphorylated tau. 3.5. Influence of renal dysfunction on plasma tau species levels Previous literature has shown that the levels of plasma p‐tau species are increased in patients with chronic kidney dysfunction. 34 , 35 We performed partial correlations between plasma tau concentrations and creatinine levels, and they were positive and significant ( p < 0.05) for all tau biomarkers, except the p‐tau217 ratio ( p = 0.193), p‐tau205 ratio ( p = 0.117), and p‐tau199 ratio (Table 3 ). This result was the same when performing multiple linear regression including adjustment for covariates (age at extraction, age at onset, time elapsed between the extraction and the death, and sex; p < 0.001). Additionally, we tested the interaction between renal dysfunction (presence/absence) and AD pathology (Braak stage) in each linear model (Table S6 in supporting information). The results showed no significant interaction effect, indicating that the influence of one variable does not depend on the level of the other. Importantly, the findings remained unchanged when these covariates were included in the analysis. TABLE 3. Association between plasma tau concentrations and creatinine levels. Ante mortem peptide concentration Spearman correlation Multiple linear regression adjustment for COV a ρ p Signif β p Signif R 2 a d j R 2 Tau 212‐221 0.46 0.000 *** 0.066 0.000 *** 0.46 0.43 Tau 195‐209 0.33 0.003 ** 0.523 0.000 *** 0.41 0.38 p‐tau 217 0.24 0.027 * 0.025 0.000 *** 0.25 0.21 p‐tau181 0.3 0.006 ** 0.77 0.000 *** 0.36 0.32 p‐tau 199 0.22 0.049 * 0.009 0.001 *** 0.19 0.14 p‐tau 202 0.28 0.010 ** 0.007 0.000 *** 0.27 0.22 p‐tau 205 0.22 0.045 * 4.4e‐04 0.000 *** 0.32 0.27 p‐tau 231 0.33 0.002 ** 0.101 0.000 *** 0.34 0.29 p‐tau 217 ratio 0.03 0.822 n.s. 0.006 0.193 n.s. 0.14 0.08 p‐tau 205 ratio 0.03 0.822 n.s. 8.3e‐05 0.117 n.s. 0.07 0.01 p‐tau 199 ratio −0.14 0.191 n.s. −0.001 0.688 n.s. 0.13 0.07 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: n.s., not significant; p‐tau, phosphorylated tau. a COV: Age at extraction, age at onset, time elapsed between the extraction and the death date, and sex. + p < 0.10. * p < 0.05. ** p < 0.01. *** p < 0.001. 3.6. Correlation of plasma tau species with brain atrophy and survival time We investigated how the plasma tau species were associated with brain atrophy by establishing correlations with brain weight in the complete sample or neuropathological subgroups: predominant AD, non‐AD, AD+, and AD+Asyn (Table S7 in supporting information). The results showed a negative and significant relationship between the levels of p‐tau199 and the brain weight (ρ = –0.23, p = 0.024) in the whole sample, but no significant correlation in the AD predominant and AD+ subgroups. Finally, we evaluated the effect of plasma tau species on the patients’ survival time (Figure 4 and Table S8 in supporting information). Multiple linear regression results showed that non‐phosphorylated tau195‐209 (β = –1.61; p = 0.015), p‐tau199 (β = –12.2; p = 0.011), and p‐tau202 (β = –15.6; p = 0.023) have a negative and significant effect on the survival time, calculated as the time elapsed between the blood collection and the date of death, only in cases diagnosed as AD predominant. This effect remained significant for tau195‐209 (β = –1.61; p = 0.038) and p‐tau199 (β = –11.6; p = 0.042) after adjustment for covariates. Additionally, after the dichotomization of the tau levels based on their quartiles, the Kaplan–Meier curve shows that AD cases in the highest quartile (Q4) of p‐tau199 ( p = 0.041) and tau195‐209 ( p = 0.002) evidenced a significantly lower survival time compared to AD patients in the lower quartiles (Q1‐Q2‐Q3; Figure S6 in supporting information). The p‐tau202 levels by quartiles did not show a statistically different survival time ( p = 0.063). FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab Effect of plasma tau species on the patients’ survival by neuropathological groups. Multiple linear regression was conducted with days elapsed between the blood extraction and death date as the dependent variable, and the standardized score of tau species as a predictor variable in independent analysis. The covariates included were disease progression (years elapsed between age at onset and age at blood extraction), age at blood extraction, and sex. Regression coefficients ordered by their magnitude in the AD‐predominant group. Significant B coefficients and their respective model‐adjusted R 2 are shown. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; p‐tau, phosphorylated tau. 4. DISCUSSION To confidently implement tau blood‐based biomarkers for AD in clinical practice and ensure accurate application and interpretation, several key questions must be addressed. These include understanding how different phosphorylated and non‐phosphorylated tau species relate to AD brain pathology and how they are affected by co‐occurring brain pathologies and peripheral comorbidities. To thoroughly answer these questions, we present data on plasma tau biomarkers measured using single‐shot MS in a cohort with confirmed neuropathological diagnoses and a very short interval between blood collection and death, minimizing the risk of misinterpreting plasma findings in relation to pathology. Our findings show that plasma tau biomarkers, particularly p‐tau217 and p‐tau205 (either alone or as a ratio), are reliable indicators of ADNC, even in the presence of other co‐existing brain pathologies. However, the relationship between plasma tau biomarkers and NFT counts varies across different brain regions. Additionally, while plasma tau biomarkers correlate with LB pathology and kidney dysfunction, these associations are reduced when using ratio‐based measures. Notably, plasma p‐tau199 and non‐phosphorylated tau emerge as strong predictors of survival time. These findings are critical for advancing the field and facilitating the application of blood‐based tau biomarkers for AD. Previous studies have shown that plasma p‐tau181 is more closely associated with neuropathological findings than with clinical diagnoses. 7 Similarly, in our study, we found no significant differences in plasma tau biomarker levels when donors were grouped by clinical diagnosis, but we did observe differences when grouped based on neuropathological classification. These findings underscore the importance of integrating blood biomarkers into the diagnostic process to support physicians in making more accurate diagnoses. Notably, the plasma p‐tau217 ratio proved to be the most effective biomarker for distinguishing between AD and non‐AD cases, AD+ and non‐AD, as well as combined AD and AD+ cases from non‐AD, followed closely by the p‐tau205 ratio. This is consistent with several previous studies that have demonstrated p‐tau217 as the most accurate and specific biomarker for detecting AD, 8 , 9 , 36 with the largest fold‐changes across the disease continuum. 37 Research on p‐tau205 has been more limited due to the lack of plasma assays targeting this marker. However, studies using the same method as in this work have shown that plasma p‐tau205 outperforms other tau species in identifying tau pathology, 14 , 15 which is in line with earlier findings from CSF studies using both MS 38 , 39 and immunoassay techniques. 40 To further investigate potential differences among plasma tau species, we evaluated the associations of each tau species with NFT counts across various brain regions: the temporal, frontal, and parietal cortices. In the full sample, most tau species showed significant associations with NFT counts in the temporal and frontal cortices. However, only p‐tau217, p‐tau205, and their respective ratios were significantly linked to NFTs in the parietal cortex. This may reflect the typical pattern of tau spreading, 41 which begins in the temporal and frontal regions, allowing most species to associate early on, while only the most sensitive biomarkers maintain associations in the later‐affected parietal cortex. In cases with high ADNC, p‐tau217 consistently showed associations with NFT counts across all regions, while p‐tau205 was primarily linked to the frontal cortex, and non‐phosphorylated tau peptides were linked to the temporal cortex, suggesting distinct patterns of emergence, progression, and clearance among these biomarkers. Interestingly, the p‐tau199 ratio displayed a unique behavior, showing significant negative associations with NFT counts in the temporal and parietal regions, contrasting with the other tau biomarkers, and suggesting a potentially different role for p‐tau199 in the disease progression. An important factor that may affect the association of plasma tau biomarkers with ADNC pathology is the presence of coexisting brain pathologies. Conditions such as CAA, TDP‐43 inclusions, and LB pathology often occur alongside AD, complicating the definitive neuropathological diagnoses. Our study found no associations between plasma tau levels and neuropathological features such as Deramecourt et al.’s vascular pathology, amyloid angiopathy intensity, or TDP‐43 deposition but revealed a significant negative association with LB pathology. Approximately 50% of dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) patients exhibit low CSF Aβ levels, 42 and there is evidence suggesting a link between the presence of AD pathology and the rate of cognitive decline in DLB. 43 , 44 , 45 Additionally, several studies have shown that p‐tau levels are elevated in the later stages of DLB compared to controls, though significantly lower than in AD. 46 , 47 , 48 , 49 In our findings, the negative association between tau blood biomarkers and LB pathology remained significant even when accounting for Braak stages. However, this effect was not observed when using phospho‐to‐non‐phospho tau ratios. Additionally, when we analyzed how plasma tau biomarker levels changed with advancing LB pathology stages, we observed a decline in plasma tau levels as LB pathology progressed, even among individuals at the same tau Braak stage. This aligns with findings from a study involving 1638 participants in the Alzheimer''s Disease Neuroimaging Initiative, in whom CSF α‐syn levels were found to be inversely associated with CSF p‐tau181 levels. 50 Previous and recent research has demonstrated distinct clinical manifestations when these pathologies coexist, 51 along with different regional patterns of glucose hypometabolism 52 and tau pathology compared to pure AD cases. 53 , 54 An explanation could be that changes in tau distribution in the brain may lead to lower levels of tau released into the CSF and blood, resulting in decreased levels compared to AD alone. Additionally, some studies have reported that protein levels in the CSF are generally lower in DLB, 55 which could explain the decrease in plasma tau biomarkers with advanced LPC stages and the lack of association with tau biomarker ratios. These factors should be carefully considered when establishing diagnostic cutoffs for AD, particularly in cases in which LB pathology is also present. Beyond the influence of co‐pathologies, peripheral conditions such as hypertension, stroke, myocardial infarction, and especially chronic kidney disease (CKD), have been shown to elevate plasma p‐tau181 and p‐tau217 levels independent of brain amyloid status. 35 The increase in p‐tau217 levels in patients with renal dysfunction appears to be mitigated by using the phospho‐to‐non‐phospho tau ratio measured by MS. 34 In cases in which reduced kidney filtration elevates the levels of all proteins, including both phosphorylated and non‐phosphorylated tau, the ratio compensates for this systemic increase. Previous studies used PET imaging to classify patients. In contrast, our study used the gold standard of neuropathological examination, confirming that blood levels of all tau biomarkers positively correlate with creatinine, a marker of kidney dysfunction. This effect was not limited to p‐tau217 but extended to all p‐tau biomarkers, with ratios such as p‐tau217, p‐tau205, and p‐tau199 effectively compensating for the influence of CKD on biomarker levels. These findings are critical for accurately interpreting blood biomarker results, particularly in patients with suspected CKD who are undergoing testing for AD diagnosis. Finally, we investigated the relationship between plasma tau biomarkers and brain weight, a proxy for neurodegeneration, as well as their association with time to death. Our analysis revealed a significant negative correlation between p‐tau199 and brain weight. Additionally, patients with the highest levels of p‐tau199 and tau195‐209 had significantly shorter survival times compared to AD patients with lower levels of these biomarkers. This may reflect the tendency of non‐phosphorylated tau species to more accurately signal neurodegeneration, particularly in the later stages of the disease. Previous studies have linked non‐phosphorylated tau species to advanced disease progression and tau PET signals; 38 , 56 however, less is known about p‐tau199. Interestingly, the observed association was driven by p‐tau199 alone, rather than its ratio to non‐phosphorylated tau, highlighting its distinct behavior compared to other tau species. This could be due to a simultaneous rise in non‐phosphorylated tau, which may reduce the utility of the ratio in these cases. These findings emphasize the need to better understand the dynamics of tau ratios across the disease continuum, especially as non‐phosphorylated tau levels increase. The main strengths of this study include the use of a unique MS method capable of simultaneously detecting multiple tau species and the inclusion of a well‐characterized neuropathological cohort with a short interval between blood collection and death. Nonetheless, several limitations should be noted. This study did not include a validation cohort due to the scarcity of other neuropathologically characterized cohorts with comparable quality and examination depth, along with sufficient paired blood samples to perform MS analysis. Additionally, the small sample size, especially in groups with co‐pathologies, limited the study''s statistical power. The low number of control cases, along with the fact that most participants were in the later stages of disease progression, also restricted our ability to investigate earlier stages of brain pathology. Finally, clinical diagnoses were made across various settings, including by general practitioners and in long‐term care facilities, which may have impacted diagnostic accuracy. In conclusion, this study has provided valuable insights into the diagnostic accuracy of different plasma tau species for AD. We demonstrated how these tau biomarkers differently correlate with NFT counts across distinct brain regions, how they are influenced by co‐pathologies and comorbidities, and their potential to predict time to death. This information is crucial for the accurate implementation and interpretation of blood‐based tau biomarkers in clinical practice. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT Henrik Zetterberg has served on scientific advisory boards and/or as a consultant for Abbvie, Acumen, Alector, Alzinova, ALZpath, Amylyx, Annexon, Apellis, Artery Therapeutics, AZTherapies, Cognito Therapeutics, CogRx, Denali, Eisai, LabCorp, Merry Life, Nervgen, Novo Nordisk, Optoceutics, Passage Bio, Pinteon Therapeutics, Prothena, Quanterix, Red Abbey Labs, reMYND, Roche, Samumed, Siemens Healthineers, Triplet Therapeutics, and Wave, has given lectures sponsored by Alzecure, BioArctic, Biogen, Cellectricon, Fujirebio, Lilly, Novo Nordisk, Roche, and WebMD, and is a co‐founder of Brain Biomarker Solutions in Gothenburg AB (BBS), which is a part of the GU Ventures Incubator Program (outside submitted work). Kaj Blennow has served as a consultant, on advisory boards, or on data monitoring committees for Abcam, Axon, BioArctic, Biogen, JOMDD/Shimadzu. Julius Clinical, Lilly, MagQu, Novartis, Ono Pharma, Pharmatrophix, Prothena, Roche Diagnostics, and Siemens Healthineers, and is a co‐founder of Brain Biomarker Solutions in Gothenburg AB (BBS), which is a part of the GU Ventures Incubator Program, outside the work presented in this study. The other authors declare no competing interests. Author disclosures are available in the Supporting Information . Supporting information Supporting Information ALZ-21-e70562-s002.pdf (3.8MB, pdf) Supporting Information ALZ-21-e70562-s001.docx (2.8MB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors wish to thank the participants of the Vallecas Alzheimer Reina Sofia study (VARS) cohort and their family members. We also want to acknowledge the personnel at the Reina Sofía Center. Laia Montoliu‐Gaya is supported by the Brightfocus Foundation (A2022015F), the Swedish Dementia Foundation, Gun and Bertil Stohnes Foundation, Åhlén‐stifelsen, Alzheimerfonden (AF‐968621), and Gamla Tjänarinnor Foundation. Kaj Blennow is supported by the Swedish Research Council (#2017‐00915), the Alzheimer Drug Discovery Foundation (ADDF), USA (#RDAPB‐201809‐2016615), the Swedish Alzheimer Foundation (#AF‐742881), Hjärnfonden, Sweden (#FO2017‐0243), the Swedish state under the agreement between the Swedish government and the County Councils, the ALF‐agreement (#ALFGBG‐715986), the European Union Joint Program for Neurodegenerative Disorders (JPND2019‐466‐236), the National Institute of Health (NIH), USA, (grant #1R01AG068398‐01), and the Alzheimer''s Association 2021 Zenith Award (ZEN‐21‐848495). Henrik Zetterberg is a Wallenberg Scholar and a Distinguished Professor at the Swedish Research Council supported by grants from the Swedish Research Council (#2023‐00356, #2022‐01018 and #2019‐02397), the European Union''s Horizon Europe research and innovation program under grant agreement No 101053962, Swedish State Support for Clinical Research (#ALFGBG‐71320), the Alzheimer Drug Discovery Foundation (ADDF), USA (#201809‐2016862), the AD Strategic Fund and the Alzheimer''s Association (#ADSF‐21‐831376‐C, #ADSF‐21‐831381‐C, #ADSF‐21‐831377‐C, and #ADSF‐24‐1284328‐C), the European Partnership on Metrology, co‐financed from the European Union''s Horizon Europe Research and Innovation Programme and by the Participating States (NEuroBioStand, #22HLT07), the Bluefield Project, Cure Alzheimer''s Fund, the Olav Thon Foundation, the Erling‐Persson Family Foundation, Familjen Rönströms Stiftelse, Stiftelsen för Gamla Tjänarinnor, Hjärnfonden, Sweden (#FO2022‐0270), the European Union''s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under the Marie Skłodowska‐Curie grant agreement No 860197 (MIRIADE), the European Union Joint Programme – Neurodegenerative Disease Research (JPND2021‐00694), the National Institute for Health and Care Research University College London Hospitals Biomedical Research Centre, and the UK Dementia Research Institute at UCL (UKDRI‐1003). There is no sponsor. The VARS study is supported by the Queen Sofia Foundation. Pascual Sánchez‐Juan is supported by grants from ISCIII (PMP22/00022 and PI23/01314) and TED2021‐131676B‐100. All brain donations were carried out under informed consent by a relative or proxy, as approved by the research ethics board of the Carlos III Research Institute. All tissues and blood samples used in the study were transferred by the local biobank (CIEN Tissue Bank) according to approved protocols. Montoliu‐Gaya L, Valeriano‐Lorenzo E, Ashton NJ, et al. Plasma tau biomarkers are distinctly associated with tau tangles and decreased with Lewy body pathology. Alzheimer''s Dement. 2025;21:e70562. 10.1002/alz.70562 Laia Montoliu‐Gaya and Elizabeth Valeriano‐Lorenzo contributed equally to this study. Kaj Blennow and Pascual Sánchez‐Juan jointly supervised this work. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT This study includes no data deposited in external repositories. Anonymized data can be shared upon reasonable request from a qualified academic investigator, for the sole purpose of replicating procedures and results presented in the article, as long as data transfer agrees with local legislation and with the local ethical review board of the cohort, which must be regulated in a material/data transfer agreement. REFERENCES 1. Hansson O. Biomarkers for neurodegenerative diseases. Nat Med. 2021;27(6):954‐963. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hansson O, Blennow K, Zetterberg H, Dage J. Blood biomarkers for Alzheimer''s disease in clinical practice and trials. Nat Aging. 2023;3(5):506‐519. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Karikari TK, Ashton NJ, Brinkmalm G, et al. Blood phospho‐tau in Alzheimer''s disease: analysis, interpretation, and clinical utility. Nat Rev (under revision). 2021. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Hansson O, Edelmayer RM, Boxer AL, et al. The Alzheimer''s Association appropriate use recommendations for blood biomarkers in Alzheimer''s disease. Alzheimers Dement. 2022;18(12):2669‐2686. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed