 stram 94102s825 claiborn aggravation stagnant nyberg gustavsson jarup bellander berglind jakobsson pershagen oglesby rotko krutli boudet kruize kunzli jea pellizzari perritt nhexas jea doring immervoll wichmann pepys augsburg euhj pikhart bobak gorynski wojtyniak danova celko kriz sulphur saviah s004200100266 arden thun calle eugenia krewski kazuhiko thurston rijnders brunekreef 01109s3411 poje detels chapa fruin beate fruin chapa guadalupe romieu meneses sienra grievink walda romieu meneses lemire colome rotko koistinen hanninen jantunen pm2 expolis jea rotko kousa alm jantunen expolis jea samet dominici curriero coursac zeger nejm200012143432401 raudenbush earls sarnat koutrakis gaseous 01109s3405 zanobetti adgate jea bailar utell samet romieu peering tolbert mulholland carlin dorley a010280 visscher seidell bouter mcquillan wener segovia sesma sergio mauricio consumo tabaco hogares encuesta ingresos gastos hogares mex s76 s81 weiland mundt ruckmann steinbach zanobetti dockery zanobetti zanobetti pm10','Abstract The effects of both ambient air pollution and socioeconomic position (SEP) on health are well documented. A limited number of recent studies suggest that SEP may itself play a role in the epidemiology of disease and death associated with exposure to air pollution. Together with evidence that poor and working-class communities are often more exposed to air pollution, these studies have stimulated discussion among scientists, policy makers, and the public about the differential distribution of the health impacts from air pollution. Science and public policy would benefit from additional research that integrates the theory and practice from both air pollution and social epidemiologies to gain a better understanding of this issue. In this article we aim to promote such research by introducing readers to methodologic and conceptual approaches in the fields of air pollution and social epidemiology; by proposing theories and hypotheses about how air pollution and socioeconomic factors may interact to influence health, drawing on studies conducted worldwide; by discussing methodologic issues in the design and analysis of studies to determine whether health effects of exposure to ambient air pollution are modified by SEP; and by proposing specific steps that will advance knowledge in this field, fill information gaps, and apply research results to improve public health in collaboration with affected communities. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (434.2 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Anderton D. L., Anderson A. B., Oakes J. M., Fraser M. R. Environmental equity: the demographics of dumping. Demography. 1994 May;31(2):229–248. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bach Jean-Francois. The effect of infections on susceptibility to autoimmune and allergic diseases. N Engl J Med. 2002 Sep 19;347(12):911–920. doi: 10.1056/NEJMra020100. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Balfour Jennifer L., Kaplan George A. Neighborhood environment and loss of physical function in older adults: evidence from the Alameda County Study. Am J Epidemiol. 2002 Mar 15;155(6):507–515. doi: 10.1093/aje/155.6.507. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bell Michelle L., Davis Devra, Cifuentes Luis, Cohen Aaron, Gouveia Nelson, Grant Lester, Green Collin, Johnson Todd, Rogat Jorge, Spengler Jack. International expert workshop on the analysis of the economic and public health impacts of air pollution: workshop summary. Environ Health Perspect. 2002 Nov;110(11):1163–1168. doi: 10.1289/ehp.021101163. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bellander T., Berglind N., Gustavsson P., Jonson T., Nyberg F., Pershagen G., Järup L. Using geographic information systems to assess individual historical exposure to air pollution from traffic and house heating in Stockholm. Environ Health Perspect. 2001 Jun;109(6):633–639. doi: 10.1289/ehp.01