INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6160,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4565605','Epidemiology''s continuing contribution to public health: The power of "Then and Now".','Buck Louis GM, Bloom MS, Gatto NM, Hogue CR, Westreich DJ, Zhang C.','2015','47th overcame relevancy tempered attracts talented 47th kirsten moysich bernardino nuns percival pott chimney sweeps pott soot pott diethylstilbestrol kirsten moysich moysich millikan moysich bread moysich moysich reasearch affair moysich cautioned siscovick cuilin siscovick seafood siscovick bouts siscovick cautioned cuilin periconception triads hogue tough hogue popularized drove definitional cautioned untoward tough tough tough tough salutogenics impoverished reminding multipronged beate cautioned industrializing polluted homegrown await warned mistimed nondifferential nonpersistent diesters perchlorate untenable pervasiveness apelberg nonsmokers publicizing banning apelberg apelberg apelberg premarket apelberg apelberg arijit nandi walked celebrated villerme farr 1800s goldberger 1900s consequentialist arijit nandi consequentialism nandi consequentialism nandi nicolle gatto remarking generals walked overemphasis remarking acyclic gatto interventionist changers interventionist exchangeability acyclic gatto interventionist formalization transportability gatto interventionist westreich westreich forays westreich interrelatedness eunice shriver germaine rensselaer rensselaer nicolle gatto mailman nicolle gatto rollins hogue gillings westreich eunice shriver cuilin eunice shriver epiresearch sprangers aaronson whosis whostat en_whs09_table2 winkelstein firsts claypon winkelstein morabia snippets hampton data_statistics sgr lagiou trichopoulou trichopoulos fradkin cowie celebrating sorlie morbis artificum diatriba pott chirurgical polypus ruptures mortification hawes ulfelder poskanzer stilbestrol millikan verhaak rgw tamayo prognostically siscovick raghunathan hennekens siscovick gissi sopravvivenza infarto kromhout giltay geleijnse schiele labarthe bellamy hingorani bramham parnell piercy g2301 hogue keck delmar cengage regil gaxiiola dowsell periconceptional cd007950 gluckman cabai tough antonovsky spiegelman adami jakobsen heitmann farvid linoleic mozaffarian katan ascherio trichopoulou costacou bamia estruch salvado salvado bullo estruch spiegelman yaun arevalo tolentino multipronged gochfeld chronologic preamble arrighi picciotto colborn nonmonotonic tails a26 a29 surgeongeneral tobaccoproducts guidancecomplianceregulatoryinformation ucm246129 mankind goldberger sydenstricker pellagra a134 consequentialist cates seize afterthought naimi maclehose misgivings blevins carinii brumback zidovudine lanoy biostat bedford cobey beerli h3n2 e1000918 azman luquero bissau bissau e1901 westreich','Abstract The 47th annual meeting of the Society for Epidemiologic Research hosted 17 invited speakers charged by the Executive Committee with presenting some of the many ways that epidemiologists have improved the health of the general population. There were 9 “Then and Now” sessions that were structured to focus on how early epidemiologists overcame research hurdles and advanced health through innovative strategies. For most topics, a longstanding expert was paired with an excellent contemporary epidemiologist working in the area, and both were given the freedom to deliver an integrated story about epidemiology''s temporal role in protecting and promoting public health. Epidemiologic discoveries in cardiovascular, cancer, and perinatal epidemiology were discussed on day 1, followed by discussions of accomplishments in reducing exposures that adversely impact health (nutrition, environment/occupation, and tobacco use) on day 2. Topics with relevancy for many aspects of epidemiology were presented on day 3, including infectious diseases, social forces, and causal thinking in epidemiologic research. Given the large number of outstanding senior and junior epidemiologists that attended the meeting, choosing speakers was a unique challenge. What became evident from all sessions was the passion that epidemiologists have for population health, tempered with concerns for remaining true to epidemiologic principles, the timely adoption of innovative methods, and the responsible interpretation of research findings. Keywords: cancer, cardiovascular epidemiology, environment, infection, nutrition, perinatal epidemiology, social epidemiology, tobacco Epidemiologists promote the health of all populations through etiologic and interventional research supported by innovative methods and the accurate interpretation of research findings. Epidemiology attracts talented individuals who appreciate the many challenges confronting our research, such as disease complexity, the many mixtures to which humans are exposed, the difficulty in motivating behavioral change, and the uneven access to and use of preventive and therapeutic clinical services. When facing such challenges, it is important to remember our discoveries that have improved life expectancy ( 1 ) and quality of life ( 2 ). Still, many environmental and social factors continue to affect the prevalence of diseases such as cancer and cardiovascular disease that account for a considerable proportion of mortality ( 3 ), as do other emerging threats such as highly caloric and poor-quality diets, use of unconventional tobacco products, and the (re)emergence of pathogens. The Society for Epidemiologic Research''s leadership realized that we have the ability to gain perspective on how best to meet contemporary challenges in maximizing population health by reflecting upon our history. Whereas past authors have described epidemiology''s history from a chronological ( 4 – 6 ), exposure ( 7 , 8 ), or disease ( 9 – 11 ) perspective, the Society had the idea to pair longstanding experts with emerging leaders to discuss past and current accomplishments and strategies for discovery. We dedicated 3 invited sessions to this topic at our 47th annual meeting, which was held on June 25–27, 2014. Although seemingly a simple task, a few issues emerged, namely which topics and speakers to select and what to call the sessions. The invited sessions were entitled “Then and Now” and included discussions on prevalent diseases (cardiovascular disease, cancer, and perinatal diseases), prevalent exposures (nutrition, occupation/environment, and tobacco smoking), and overarching considerations (social forces, causal thinking, and infectious diseases). In the present article, we describe the speakers'' key points, as adapted from their talks. “THEN AND NOW” SESSIONS Cancer Drs. Scott Davis and Kirsten Moysich opened the first Then and Now session on day 1 with cancer epidemiology. Dr. Davis provided an historical overview of cancer epidemiology''s beginning in the 18th century, followed by discussion of areas of scholarship and research. Dr. Davis tasked us with answering the questions of how well the past informs our research and how well we do or do not learn from our successes and failures. He reminded us about some of the earliest notable achievements in cancer epidemiology, such as Bernardino Ramazzini''s discovery in 1713 that nuns had virtually no incidence of cervical cancer but a high incidence of breast cancer ( 12 ). These early observations shed light on the hormonal and infectious influences on cancer etiology. Percival Pott reported in 1775 that chimney sweeps had high rates of scrotal cancer ( 13 ). A unique feature of Pott''s work was the use of comparison populations, which led to the discovery of the role of soot in scrotal cancer. Dr. Davis also noted that Pott''s work might be considered the first occupational epidemiologic study and the early origin of protective workplace clothing. He noted that identification of the link between tobacco smoking and lung cancer is likely the landmark accomplishment that led to the eventual reduction in global smoking rates ( 14 ); the association between diethylstilbestrol and adenocarcinoma was another key discovery ( 15 ). Dr. Davis concluded that the inclusion of basic research into traditional epidemiologic research is likely to lead to continued discoveries. Dr. Kirsten Moysich then discussed the considerable heterogeneity of cancer, even among site-specific cancers. Dr. Moysich emphasized that cancer comprises many diseases with distinct etiologies, incidence rates, treatments, and prognoses. She reminded us of the late Robert Millikan''s important work published in 2008 in which intrinsic subtypes of breast cancer in relation to reproductive risk factors were first assessed ( 16 ). This work demonstrated the importance of molecular signatures rather than reliance on organ-specific tumors for understanding cancer etiology. Dr. Moysich spoke about the role of case-control studies, the so-called “bread and butter” of cancer epidemiology research. However, she noted that the decreasing participation rates, especially for controls, resulted in increasing concern about selection bias. In addition, Dr. Moysich discussed how the growing focus on genomic risk factors or susceptibilities led to an increasing role of cancer consortia for epidemiologic research, which are now available for every major cancer site. Dr. Moysich described how as we move forward, cancer consortia are fine-tuning their representations of population subgroups (e.g., African Americans and persons with mutations of the breast cancer susceptibility genes ( BRCA1 and BRCA2 ) ( 17 ). Given cancer reasearch''s long love affair with genetics, consortia provide an opportunity to move from studies of single nucleotide polymorphisms to genome-wide association studies. Dr. Moysich cautioned that well-designed individual studies are still needed and will play an important role in identifying modifiable risk factors across the lifespan that are amenable to intervention. Cardiovascular disease Drs. David Siscovick and Cuilin Zhang presented the session on cardiovascular disease. Dr. Siscovick introduced the field''s legacy, noting that coronary heart disease encompasses various diseases and that research over the past 4 decades has demonstrated that certain risk factors are unique to particular cardiovascular outcomes, which underscores the importance of the specificity of outcomes. For example, a greater dietary intake of long-chain n -3 polyunsaturated fatty acids from seafood is associated with lower risks of sudden cardiac arrest and fatal coronary heart disease, but it is not associated with nonfatal myocardial infarction ( 18 , 19 ). Dr. Siscovick also noted that a single exposure, such as vigorous physical activity, could have both risks and benefits. Both the timing (during or in addition to vigorous exercise) and the dose (habitual level) of the exposure are important for observed coronary heart disease risks. For example, among more physically active persons, although there is a transient increase in the risk of sudden cardiac arrest during acute bouts of exercise, the risk is lower at other times, yielding a lower risk overall ( 20 ). Lastly, Dr. Siscovick cautioned about the influence of cohort effects when weighing evidence across studies. He noted that inconsistent findings from large, randomized, double-blind clinical trials that focused on the same question, such as the efficacy of long-chain n -3 supplementation in secondary prevention after myocardial infarction, during different calendar (time) periods might reflect the influence of cohort effects due to secular trends in lifestyle (e.g., diet), therapies, and procedures on the associations of interest ( 21 – 23 ). Dr. Cuilin Zhang discussed the growing weight of evidence supporting the importance of exposures during the periconception and in utero sensitive windows for cardiometabolic outcomes. She discussed why such windows are important for the primary prevention of cardiovascular disease, the so-called next frontier of modifiable cardiovascular disease risk ( 24 ). Dr. Zhang noted that some pregnancy complications, such as preeclampsia, are associated with increased cardiovascular disease risk with remarkably consistent findings and suggested that pregnancy complications might be early indicators of later-onset cardiovascular disease ( 25 , 26 ). Dr. Zhang noted that future transdisciplinary initiatives might utilize family-based triads to study transgenerational impacts by measuring risk across the lifespan and “omics” approaches to comprehensively characterize in utero etiologic exposures and mechanisms. A recurrent theme of Dr. Zhang''s talk was the need for early and continual promotion of cardiometabolic health during sensitive windows of human development. Perinatal epidemiology Drs. Carol Hogue and Suzanne Tough presented the perinatal epidemiology session. Dr. Hogue reminded us that what''s old is new again, as evidenced by a renewed focus on the importance of intergenerational effects that was first popularized by Helen Wallace in 1982 ( 27 ) and on the programs that reduced the impact of poverty and inequality on pregnancy outcomes and drove the reduction in infant and maternal mortality rates in the early 20th century ( 28 ). Since the mid-20th century, epidemiologists have improved clinical and etiologic understanding of the heterogeneity of perinatal outcomes through definitional improvements in categories of birth weight and gestational age. Preterm delivery, including stillbirth, has become the major focus for perinatal outcomes research, with hypotheses related to exposures during pregnancy, perhaps as mediated through stress, nutritional status, and infections ( 29 ). She cautioned, however, that prenatal intervention might be too late to improve outcomes; change could require prevention or treatment before conception. Thus, women''s health before pregnancy is the key strategy for improving perinatal health and minimizing adverse outcomes, as evidenced by the use of folic acid supplementation for the prevention of neural tube defects ( 30 ), the availability of safe and effective contraceptives for reducing unwanted pregnancies ( 31 ), and preconception care to mitigate chronic conditions that may have untoward implications for the pregnant woman and her fetus ( 32 ). Dr. Tough also emphasized the need to consider a life course approach for research focused on perinatal outcomes such as preterm birth given that many outcomes are associated with later risk, as conceptualized in the early origins of the health and disease paradigm ( 33 ). Dr. Tough suggested that the exposome approach, which takes into account the totality of exposures ( 34 ) during sensitive windows and over time, is warranted and requires analytic competency with complex data structures. Other exposures, such as biopsychosocial, genetic, and contextual data, need to be assessed to allow for accurate interpretation of exposure-outcome relations. Given the increasing prevalence of assisted reproductive technologies, Dr. Tough called for epidemiologists to “ask women how they got pregnant and to look at the ways this may affect pregnancy and children''s development.” Lastly, Dr. Tough urged consideration of resilience ( 35 ) and salutogenics ( 36 ) when trying to understand children''s ability to develop successfully despite adversity and environmental challenges. For example, social support interventions for impoverished mothers might reduce children''s risks of adverse developmental outcomes. Such research requires innovative design and partnerships with other sciences, as well as analytic techniques that move beyond the traditional point estimates that might no longer be informative. Nutrition The first Then and Now session on day 2 was presented by Drs. Walter Willett and Whitney Robinson. Dr. Willett reflected on the role of observational designs in nutritional epidemiology, reminding us that all study designs have strengths and limitations, with the case-control design being prone to serious bias. Although measurement error is inevitable, current dietary assessment methods are sufficient for detecting most important associations. Though large randomized trials focused on diet and cancer incidence are of interest, there are reasons that they might fail, such as poor adherence and a limited duration of follow-up. As such, the best evidence will likely come from replicated cohort studies in combination with short-term trials using intermediate biomarkers. Dr. Willett noted that despite the challenges, there have been many findings about nutrition, including the importance of diet quality versus diet quantity for health. For instance, it is not the percentage of calories from fat but rather the type of fat that is a major risk factor for cancer or cardiovascular disease, as evidenced by the fact that a higher intake of polyunsaturated fats is associated with a lower risk of heart disease, whereas trans -fat intake is associated with a higher risk ( 37 – 40 ). Other recent discoveries include the facts that sugar-sweetened beverages contribute to weight gain and the risk of diabetes ( 41 ), the Mediterranean diet reduces the risk of both cardiovascular disease and diabetes ( 42 – 44 ), and alcohol consumption increases the risk of breast and other cancers ( 45 ). Dr. Robinson began by acknowledging nutrition''s “do''s and don''ts,” which can be confusing for scientists and the public alike. She discussed some of the key reasons for conflicting evidence, including the challenges in establishing cause and effect, the complexity of human behavior and its appropriate measurement, and limited theory-guided translation, such as disparity research. Assessing nutrition requires recognition that we don''t eat in a vacuum, which is why it can be hard to establish cause and effect. Dr. Robinson suggested strategies for moving forward that included the need to minimize confounding and selection bias when assessing causality and to identify upstream drivers of exposures for measurement, as well as the need for health disparity research to be grounded within conceptual frameworks. Such approaches for health disparity are being tried in multipronged community and social- or network-based strategies ( 46 ). Occupational and environmental epidemiology Drs. Beate Ritz and Michael Bloom presented the occupational and environmental epidemiology session. Dr. Ritz described the longstanding recognition that occupational and environmental exposures affect human health and disease, as first documented in the writings of Hippocrates in the 4th century BC and later by Ramazzini in the 17th century ( 47 ). She noted that epidemiologic studies of workers are a prominent source of evidence used to establish carcinogenic risks to humans, as cited by the International Agency for Research on Cancer ( 48 ), and that “dirty jobs with heavy and long-term exposures” helped epidemiologic pioneers establish causes, mechanisms, and interventions for many diseases. Dr. Ritz described how documented and detailed exposures in some industries continue to inspire and support epidemiologic research, including the development of sophisticated methods involving the treatment of time-varying exposures, lag structures, multiple health outcomes, and the exploration of complex causal structures in longitudinal data such as the healthy-worker selection bias ( 49 ). She noted that currently, many occupational exposures in high-resource economies (e.g., ergonomic and diurnal rhythm disturbances) have become less visible and harder to measure. She further cautioned that traditional exposures at low doses might still pose a risk, such as those reported for lead and agricultural pesticides with regard to neurodegenerative disorders ( 50 ). Also, occupational epidemiology remains critical as quickly industrializing countries expose large populations of workers in extended families who live in proximity to polluted industrial or homegrown production sites. Dr. Ritz concluded that many opportunities await future epidemiologists in using modern environmental monitoring, sophisticated models (gene × environment), and record linkages to investigate and warn against exposures. Dr. Bloom focused on the challenges underlying the identification and quantification of exposures, some of which are invisible. He emphasized the importance of capturing the timing of exposure with regard to biologic plausibility and detecting transient effects that occur during sensitive windows (in utero, childhood, adolescence). Dr. Bloom warned that mistimed exposure assessments might lead to null results secondary to nondifferential misclassification associated with within-individual variability. He emphasized that the dose and the timing of the dose make the poison. Dr. Bloom also noted that pervasive exposures to nonpersistent compounds can lead to so-called “pseudo-persistence” over long intervals. Exposure to agents such as bisphenol A, phthalate diesters, arsenic, and perchlorate are widespread in many populations, with ongoing doses experienced before clearance of previous doses. Strategies that compare singular acute exposures or “exposed” populations with “unexposed” populations are often untenable, given the long-term and widespread nature of some exposures. The pervasiveness of environmental exposures, including in the US population, is corroborated by near-universal detection of pollutants in human biomonitoring studies ( 51 ). Other challenges include modeling low-dose and nonlinear effects in relation to health ( 52 ). Mixtures are another challenge in understanding the impacts of environmental risks on human health and in the development of targeted public health interventions. The exposome paradigm ( 53 ) is useful for such study, though cautious interpretation of findings is needed when extrapolating to various study populations ( 54 ). He reminded us that effects that appear nonclinical in population samples might be significant when applied to large population groups—another likely consequence of widespread ongoing exposures ( 54 ). Tobacco/smoking Drs. Gary Friedman and Benjamin Apelberg presented the tobacco/smoking session. Dr. Friedman noted that observational epidemiologic research played a formidable role in the identification of the harmful health effects associated with tobacco smoking. He presented the diseases judged to be caused by smoking in the first (1964) Surgeon General''s Report ( 55 ) and the many others added in the latest (2014) Surgeon General''s Report ( 56 ) based on the accomplishments in an additional 50 years of research. Despite arguments that smokers differ in many ways from nonsmokers, such as by genotype ( 57 ) and personality traits ( 58 ), Dr. Friedman noted that no characteristic has been shown to have disease-causing effects of sufficient magnitude to explain those associated with cigarette smoking. Continued publicizing of such health effects resulted in a great societal reduction in the prevalence of smoking and a reduction in the incidence of lung and other cancers. A second key epidemiologic contribution was the identification of the health effects associated with second-hand smoke on children and adults, which provided further evidence for banning cigarette smoking in public places ( 59 ). Dr. Apelberg opened the Now session with remarks about epidemiology''s instrumental role in the accumulation of the massive body of scientific evidence that demonstrates the wide range of diseases caused by smoking, the reductions in risk and gains in life expectancy due to smoking cessation, and the disease and mortality burden attributable to smoking in populations over time ( 56 ). Dr. Apelberg noted the wide and increasing range of tobacco products currently on the market, particularly as cigarette smoking declines, and the need for descriptive epidemiologic research that includes novel surveillance methods and data sources to monitor trends in use and their implications for disease risk. One example he noted is the rise in the use of electronic cigarettes, and he highlighted the need for epidemiologic research to assess health impacts for both users and the population at large. Dr. Apelberg described the major change in tobacco control that occurred when the US Food and Drug Administration was granted regulatory authority over the manufacture, marketing, and distribution of tobacco products in 2009 ( 60 ). This oversight includes the authority to mandate changes in tobacco products to protect public health and the requirement for premarket authorization before a company can introduce a new tobacco product for marketing in the United States. Dr. Apelberg noted that this population health focus requires a multidisciplinary approach that includes a key role for epidemiologic evidence in informing and evaluating the impact of regulatory actions. Dr. Apelberg ended by reinforcing the need for novel epidemiologic approaches pertaining to causal inference given the multilevel, time-varying influences of tobacco use behavior and disease risk. Social forces The first session on day 3 focused on social forces, with Drs. Patricia O''Campo and Arijit Nandi presenting. Dr. O''Campo walked us through the celebrated examples of research from the early 19th century through the later half of the 20th century that underscored the longstanding role of social determinants on health. These included the notable contributions of Villerme and Virchow and Farr''s research in the 1800s implicating poverty in poor health and examining social class gradients, as discussed by Porter ( 61 ), and Goldberger''s investigation of poverty and social conditions in the 1900s ( 62 ). Dr. O''Campo further noted that too few epidemiologic data sets contain data on the structural determinants of health. Consequently, few analytic methods have been developed to incorporate these structural determinants of health inequality into epidemiologic research. In addition, Dr. O''Campo noted that epidemiology and public health more globally are lacking social theories regarding the causes of the causes that impact the development of interventions focused on social forces and health inequalities. Dr. O''Campo concluded with a consequentialist theme, noting that that applied social epidemiologists must partner with policy makers or program planners to identify and understand the social determinants of health. Dr. Arijit Nandi began the Now session with a reminder of the many previous calls for consequentialism in epidemiology ( 63 – 65 ). He noted that this is an important message for social epidemiology, a field tasked with identifying solutions for improving population health and reducing health inequalities. Dr. Nandi noted that despite several prominent social epidemiologists calling for us to produce more evidence of the effects of existing interventions, few such initiatives are being practiced in the study of social forces. He presented results of a recent analysis that was focused on the extent to which consequentialism is being practiced in social epidemiology. In this work, investigators examined all abstracts related to social forces that were accepted at the Society for Epidemiologic Research''s annual meetings between 2009 and 2013. Dr. Nandi noted that only 6.6% of the research contained in the abstracts was deemed consequential, prompting him to offer suggestions for how social epidemiology could be more consequential (e.g., greater attention to study population''s socioeconomic and political context). Causal thinking Drs. Charles Poole and Nicolle Gatto addressed causal thinking and its implications for epidemiologic research. Dr. Poole''s talk encompassed several elements: evaluation of causal associations, consensus, general causal models, interaction, mediation, attribution, and intervention. Dr. Poole opened with remarks about the meaning and interpretation of causal thinking while remarking about the increasing usage of the term “causal” over time. Dr. Poole also noted the absence of important considerations when assessing causality (e.g., chance, bias, confounding). Dr. Poole cited examples as to how causal consensus can be reached in publications from the International Agency for Research on Cancer and the Surgeon Generals'' Advisory Committees. He then walked us through some general causal models, raising concern about epidemiology''s overemphasis on techniques and methods, continued reliance on risk factors without considering up- and down-stream factors, and black box epidemiology. While remarking about directed acyclic graphs, Dr. Poole closed with remarks about a continued need for interventional research for the promotion of population health. Dr. Gatto described the present as the era of “estimating intervention effects.” She noted that as epidemiologic methods and tools have become more sophisticated, some researchers have begun to think that epidemiology is too far removed from improving public health. From this view, these researchers encourage us to consider an interventionist approach for moving away from causal identification ( 66 , 67 ). Methodological tools, largely driven by several game changers, have been articulated in a way that connects epidemiologic studies to interventionist goals. The development of tools to achieve exchangeability, such as directed acyclic graphs and marginal structural models ( 68 – 70 ), have necessitated the articulation of additional conditions for estimating intervention effects, such as positivity and various types of causal effects. Dr. Gatto predicted that as calls for interventionist epidemiology continue, we would see further development and formalization of tools to evaluate the transportability of causal effects estimated from observational studies. Dr. Gatto concluded that this interventionist perspective might better improve public health through integration with causal identification. Infectious diseases The final talk on the third day focused on epidemiologic investigations of infectious diseases. Dr. Daniel Westreich presented the Now talk with some attention to past accomplishments. He began by describing the last 25 years of infectious disease epidemiology as an era marked by critical scientific questions that led to the development of new and innovative research methods and tools. The question of when to start antiretroviral therapy among individuals infected with human immunodeficiency virus led to the refinement of and extensions to the g-methods of Robins and et al. ( 70 – 73 ), which are now finding applications in other substantive areas. Questions about infectious disease transmission, spread, and prevention prompted the development of sophisticated mathematical models that routinely integrated “big data” in the form of time-varying social and sexual network structures, statistical distributions of infectious contacts, and spatial and phylogenetic data ( 74 , 75 ). Dr. Westreich also noted the need to evaluate the real-world effectiveness of interventions for preventing further spread and impact of human immunodeficiency virus and other infectious diseases and to develop methods with applicability to the development of innovative approaches for the design and analysis of clinical trials. He noted that these were initial forays of epidemiology into implementation science ( 76 ). Dr. Westreich concluded with remarks about how infectious diseases epidemiology will take a central role in confronting at least 2 key public health challenges in the next century, namely antimicrobial resistance and changing patterns of infectious disease linked to global climate change. CONCLUDING REMARKS FROM THEN AND NOW SESSIONS Over the 9 Then and Now sessions, several key themes emerged. Still, considerably more work needs to be done in light of (re)emerging exposures and diseases. Such work includes the need for greater recognition of the multifactorial causes of disease ranging from macro (societal, neighborhood) to micro (molecular) factors, the capture of exposures during sensitive windows, and a better understanding of the interrelatedness of disease states across the lifespan. As is evident from the above highlights, epidemiologists can delve further into the conceptual frameworks that are reshaping our thinking (e.g., exposome and early origins of health and disease) and their respective methods. Although much remains to be done—in particular promoting health while eliminating health disparities—our field is well served by committed, capable professionals determined to achieve their mission. Their discoveries have improved public health while continuing to inform future research. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS Author affiliations: Office of the Director, Division of Intramural Population Health Research, Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development, Rockville, Maryland (Germaine M. Buck Louis); Department of Environmental Health Sciences, School of Public Health, University of Albany, State University of New York, Rensselaer, New York (Michael S. Bloom); Department of Epidemiology and Biostatistics, School of Public Health, University of Albany, State University of New York, Rensselaer, New York (Michael S. Bloom); Epidemiology, Worldwide Safety and Regulatory, Pfizer Inc., New York, New York (Nicolle M. Gatto); Department of Epidemiology, Mailman School of Public Health, Columbia University, New York, New York (Nicolle M. Gatto); Department of Epidemiology, Rollins School of Public Health, Emory University, Atlanta, Georgia (Carol R. Hogue); Department of Epidemiology, Gillings School of Global Public Health, University of North Carolina—Chapel Hill, Chapel Hill, North Carolina (Daniel J. Westreich); and Epidemiology Branch, Division of Intramural Population Health Research, Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development, Rockville, Maryland (Cuilin Zhang). This work was supported in part by the Intr