ported human Campylobacter infections in Scotland. BMC Public Health. 2010;10:423. doi: 10.1186/1471-2458-10-423. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Mullner P, Shadbolt T, Collins-Emerson JM, Midwinter AC, Spencer SE, Marshall J, Carter PE, Campbell DM, Wilson DJ, Hathaway S. et al. Molecular and spatial epidemiology of human campylobacteriosis: source association and genotype-related risk factors. Epidemiol Infect. 2010;138(10):1372–1383. doi: 10.1017/S0950268809991579. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Wilson DJ, Gabriel E, Leatherbarrow AJ, Cheesbrough J, Gee S, Bolton E, Fox A, Fearnhead P, Hart CA, Diggle PJ. Tracing the source of campylobacteriosis. PLoS Genet. 2008;4(9):e1000203. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1000203. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ogden ID, Dallas JF, MacRae M, Rotariu O, Reay KW, Leitch M, Thomson AP, Sheppard SK, Maiden M, Forbes KJ. et al. Campylobacter excreted into the environment by animal sources: prevalence, concentration shed, and host association. Foodborne Pathog Dis. 2009;6(10):1161–1170. doi: 10.1089/fpd.2009.0327. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Carstairs V, Morris R. Deprivation and health in Scotland. Health Bull (Edinb) 1990;48(4):162–175. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] UKBorders Service. http://www.edina.ac.uk/ Bates D, Maechler M, Bin D. lme4: Linear mixed-effects models using S4 classes. 2011. R Development Core Team. R: A language and environment for statistical computing. Vienna, Austria: R Foundation for Statistical Computing; 2008. [ Google Scholar ] French NP, Midwinter A, Holland B, Collins-Emerson J, Pattison R, Colles F, Carter P. Molecular epidemiology of Campylobacter jejuni isolates from wild-bird fecal material in children''s playgrounds. Appl Environ Microbiol. 2009;75(3):779–783. doi: 10.1128/AEM.01979-08. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Strachan NJ, Gormley FJ, Rotariu O, Ogden ID, Miller G, Dunn GM, Sheppard SK, Dallas JF, Reid TM, Howie H. et al. Attribution of campylobacter infections in northeast Scotland to specific sources by use of multilocus sequence typing. J Infect Dis. 2009;199(8):1205–1208. doi: 10.1086/597417. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Sopwith W, Birtles A, Matthews M, Fox A, Gee S, Painter M, Regan M, Syed Q, Bolton E. Identification of potential environmentally adapted Campylobacter jejuni strain, United Kingdom. Emerg Infect Dis. 2008;14(11):1769–1773. doi: 10.3201/eid1411.071678. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Rind E, Pearce J. The spatial distribution of campylobacteriosis in New Zealand, 1997-2005. Epidemiol Infect. 2010;138(10):1359–1371. doi: 10.1017/S095026881000018X. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] McCarthy N, Giesecke J. Case-case comparisons to study causation of common infectious diseases. Int J Epidemiol. 1999;28(4):764–768. doi: 10.1093/ije/28.4.764. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Gillespie IA, O''Brien SJ, Frost JA, Adak GK, Horby P, Swan AV, Painter MJ, Neal KR. A case-case comparison of Campylobacter coli and Campylobacter jejuni infection: a tool for generating hypotheses. Emerg Infect Dis. 2002;8(9):937–942. doi: 10.3201/eid0809.10.3201/eid0809.010187. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',27,'2025-10-20 05:49:18','2025-10-20 05:49:18');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6199,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2214623','Complementary therapy use by cancer patients. Physicians'' perceptions, attitudes, and ideas.','O''Beirne M, Verhoef M, Paluck E, Herbert C.','2004','altshuler okla bourgeault corbin winslow psychophysiol munstedt entezami wartenberg kullmer uct s0959','Abstract OBJECTIVE: To explore family physicians'' perceptions of their cancer patients'' use of complementary therapy. DESIGN: Qualitative pilot study. SETTING: British Columbia and Alberta. PARTICIPANTS: Rural and urban family physicians. METHOD: Five focus groups were conducted with a total of 28 participants. Content analysis of focus group transcripts. MAIN FINDINGS: Eight themes were identified: definition of complementary therapies, importance of holistic health, role of evidence, attitudes toward complementary therapies, perceptions of cancer patients'' use of complementary therapies, patient-physician communication, perceptions of family physicians'' role with respect to complementary therapies, and concerns about complementary therapies. Family physicians believed that many of their patients were using complementary therapies and that patients and physicians needed to communicate about this practice. CONCLUSION: The study increased understanding of physicians''perspectives on communication about complementary therapies and exposed issues that need to be addressed through education and research. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (199.5 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Adams J. General practitioners, complementary therapies and evidence-based medicine: the defence of clinical autonomy. Complement Ther Med. 2000 Dec;8(4):248–252. doi: 10.1054/ctim.2000.0396. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Altshuler L. H. Alternative medicine: what is the physician''s role? J Okla State Med Assoc. 1999 May;92(5):219–226. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Astin J. A., Marie A., Pelletier K. R., Hansen E., Haskell W. L. A review of the incorporation of complementary and alternative medicine by mainstream physicians. Arch Intern Med. 1998 Nov 23;158(21):2303–2310. doi: 10.1001/archinte.158.21.2303. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Barry M. J. Involving patients in medical decisions: how can physicians do better? JAMA. 1999 Dec 22;282(24):2356–2357. doi: 10.1001/jama.282.24.2356. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bourgeault I. L. Physicians'' attitudes toward patients'' use of alternative cancer therapies. CMAJ. 1996 Dec 15;155(12):1679–1685. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Corbin Winslow Lisa, Shapiro Howard. Physicians want education about complementary and alternative medicine to enhance communication with their patients. Arch Intern Med. 2002 May 27;162(10):1176–1181. doi: 10.1001/archinte.162.10.1176. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Coulter A. Partnerships with patients: the pros and cons of shared clinical decision-making. J Health Serv Res Policy. 1997 Apr;2(2):112–121. doi: 10.1177/135581969700200209. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Jonas W. B. Advising patients on the use of complementary and alternative medicine. Appl Psychophysiol Biofeedback. 2001 Sep;26(3):205–214. doi: 10.1023/a:1011398120476. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Kaptchuk T. J., Eisenberg D. M. Varieties of healing. 2: a taxonomy of unconventional healing practices. Ann Intern Med. 2001 Aug 7;135(3):196–204. doi: 10.7326/0003-4819-135-3-200108070-00012. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Münstedt K., Entezami A., Wartenberg A., Kullmer U. The attitudes of physicians and oncologists towards unconventional cancer therapies (UCT). Eur J Cancer. 2000 Oct;36(16):2090–2095. doi: 10.1016/s0959-8049(00)00194-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Owen D. K., Lewith G., Stephens C. R. Can doctors respond to patients'' increasing interest in complementary and alternative medicine? BMJ. 2001 Jan 20;322(7279):154–158. doi: 10.1136/bmj.322.7279.154. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Stewart M. A. Effective physician-patient communication and health outcomes: a review. CMAJ. 1995 May 1;152(9):1423–1433. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Tonelli M. R. The philosophical limits of evidence-based medicine. Acad Med. 1998 Dec;73(12):1234–1240. doi: 10.1097/00001888-199812000-00011. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Verhoef M. J., Sutherland L. R. Alternative medicine and general practitioners. Opinions and behaviour. Can Fam Physician. 1995 Jun;41:1005–1011. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Verhoef M. J., White M. A., Doll R. Cancer patients'' expectations of the role of family physicians in communication about complementary therapies. Cancer Prev Control. 1999 Jun;3(3):181–187. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',11,'2025-10-20 05:49:22','2025-10-20 05:49:22');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6200,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9191304','Integrating Medical Genetics Into Precision Oncology Practice in the Veterans Health Administration: The Time Is Now.','Scott A, Mohan A, Austin S, Amini E, Raupp S, Pannecouk B, Kelley MJ, Narla G, Ramnath N.','2022','ppgvs aahs ppgvs npop aahs ppgv npop ppgvs ppgv ppgvs ppgvs underrecognized npop npop ppgvs ppgv ppgvs ppgv chek2 ppgv flcn birt npop npop foundationone cdx pgdx ppgv ppgv ppgv ppgv ppgvs rad51c rad51d palb2 mutyh ppgv ppgvs ppgv palb2 ppgv ppgvs ppgvs npop ppgvs ppgv ppgv ppgv ppgv ppgvs ppgv ppgvs nontumor ppgvs ppgv foundationone cdx ppgvs ppgv vamcs npop vamc vamcs alternately phlebotomy shipped vamcs ppgv arathi viatris openpaymentsdata nithya ramnath clovis clovis lpop popcap shelby raupp brittany pannecouk goutham narla erika amini shelby raupp brittany pannecouk arathi goutham narla nithya ramnath rwc ascopubs arathi viatris openpaymentsdata nithya ramnath clovis clovis schwaederle chakravarty solit meric bernstam brusco genomically fojo schilsky schrader nussbaum kurian e2019452 mandelker talukdar esplin deleonardis cannistra schaeffer srinivas antonarakis returnurl physician_gls stuttgen croessmann fetting poonnen hintze scheuner myrie peredo 37s82 s882020 mandelker kemel ponti castellsague ruini lener kluzniak bytnar moncur usab291 milmed usab291 losa pane selitsky sanford e1005965 mullane schnettler cobain vats dungey dalby mainstreamed kouri elliot strohbehn dedinsky teleoncology 17e1362 e13742021','Abstract PURPOSE: Increased access and utilization of tumor profiling of cancers in our veteran population uncovered a modest number of potentially pathogenic germline variants (PPGVs) that require genetics referral for follow-up evaluation and germline sequencing. Challenges identified specific to the veteran population include paucity of genetics providers, either at a veteran''s VA facility or nearby non-VA facilities. We sought to investigate the number of veterans who would benefit from having such resources at both local and national levels. METHODS: Annotated clinical reports of mutations identified by tumor-only profiling and medical records of veterans with solid tumors at the Veterans Administration Ann Arbor Healthcare System (VA AAHS) between 2015 and 2020 were reviewed. PPGVs were identified according to society recommendations (such as ESMO and American Board of Medical Genetics and Genomics), expert review, and/or previously published criteria. After the analysis of our local VA population, these same criteria were then applied to veterans in the National Precision Oncology Program (NPOP). RESULTS: Two hundred eight veterans underwent tumor profiling at the VA AAHS over the defined time period. This included 20 different primary tumor sites with over half (n = 130) being advanced cancer at diagnosis. Of these, 18 veterans (8.5%) had mutations suggestive of a PPGV. Applying these criteria to the larger NPOP database (n = 20,014), a similar percentage (6%) of PPGVs were identified. CONCLUSION: These results indicate a PPGV frequency (6%-9% of veterans) consistent with the prevalence of inherited cancer predisposition syndromes in the general population, underscoring the need for medical genetics as part of standard oncologic care for veterans. We explore current and future care delivery models to optimize incorporation of medical genetics and genetic counseling to best serve veterans needing such services. INTRODUCTION Tumor-only sequencing is being increasingly used to tailor cancer treatments, understand disease course, and identify potential therapeutic resistance. 1 - 4 Tumor-only sequencing, however, may also identify potentially pathogenic germline variants (PPGVs), which have important implications for cancer surveillance and cancer recurrence risk for patients and their families. Previous work has shown that most PPGVs associated with cancer risk recovered from the germline are also present in the tumor. 5 , 6 Many groups and institutions have proposed various criteria 7 - 10 to determine which variants in the tumor may warrant germline confirmation. Such variants occur most often in genes associated with high-penetrance cancer predisposition syndromes or are founder variants in well-described tumor predisposition genes. These mutations have a demonstrable effect on clinical management and can inform cancer treatment and surveillance recommendations for the patient and affected family members. If not guided by a suspicious personal or family history of cancer, genetic counseling and germline testing is standard of care for only certain tumor types, such as ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancers per National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) guidelines. 11 - 14 Veterans who require oncologic care are often excluded from large-scale cancer studies, and the extent of need for clinical genetics services for this population may be underrecognized. 15 , 16 The National Precision Oncology Program (NPOP) has increased the availability of tumor profiling in the veteran population, resulting in improved oncologic care at Veterans Affairs (VA) health care facilities. This initiative involves 126 VA facilities, and to date, over 20,000 tumors have been sent for testing. 15 In addition to access to tumor profiling, this nationwide initiative also includes clinical trial enrollment, coordination among oncologists in a monthly molecular tumor board, a cancer research registry, and pharmacogenomic testing. The corresponding genetic counseling, however, associated with this testing may not be immediately available. This highlights one of the several challenges for the VA, specifically the lack of genetic counseling services on a national scale, resulting in long wait times even when these services are available and the need for referral to non-VA providers when they are not. Additionally, depending on the location of the veteran, non-VA genetics resources may be just as limited as well. 15 Using data collected as part of the NPOP at both the single-institution and nationwide levels, we sought to determine how many veterans have PPGVs on the basis of tumor-only sequencing to specifically characterize the scope of the unmet need for cancer genetics services within the VA. METHODS PPGV Criteria Criteria used to identify PPGVs have been previously described and are summarized in Table 1 . 7 - 9 , 18 , 19 Additional criteria on the basis of consensus experience from our single institution were also included. PPGV criteria are divided by (1) gene (any pathogenic variant recovered in genes associated with high-penetrance cancer predisposition syndromes, such as Lynch syndrome or hereditary breast and ovarian cancer), (2) founder variant (specific variants that are more likely to be germline in genes associated with cancer risk, but frequently undergo somatic mutation in cancer, such as ATM and CHEK2 ), and (3) clinical presentation (suspicion for a PPGV is associated only with specific tumor types, such as FLCN , associated with Birt-Hogg-Dubé syndrome and non–clear cell renal cell carcinoma). TABLE 1. Criteria Used to Identify Potentially Pathogenic Germline Variants on Tumor-Only Sequencing Reports From Solid Tumors Open in a new tab Study Population The study population includes veterans who underwent solid tumor-only sequencing as part of their oncologic care at the VA Ann Arbor Healthcare System between 2015 and 2020 (covered financially as part of routine VA medical services). For the NPOP data set, this included veterans enrolled in NPOP and had sequencing completed from January 1, 2015, to March 26, 2021. Primarily, these tumor samples were processed by Foundation Medicine (specifically their FoundationOne CDx platform), but testing was performed by Personal Genome Diagnostics (PGDx) for cases before 2019, both of which being College of American Pathologists and Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments–approved testing. These panels included between 64 and 324 genes, all of which included most PPGV candidate genes, with goal coverage up to 500×. 15 The PPGV criteria were then applied to the abstracted data to determine need for genetic counseling referral. RESULTS Description of a Local Cohort of PPGV This cohort includes 208 veterans with advanced, metastatic disease who underwent tumor-only sequencing as part of their oncologic care at the VA Ann Arbor Healthcare System, representing 20 different tumor types (Table 2 ). Most of these veterans were male (97.1%) and primarily were patients with either lung or prostate cancers. Of this cohort, 130 veterans were initially diagnosed at an advanced stage of disease (original stage at diagnosis: 23—stage I, 24—stage II, 37—stage III, and 124—stage IV). TABLE 2. Clinical Characteristics of the Veterans Being Studied Open in a new tab Identification of PPGV Applying the criteria described above resulted in 22 PPGVs identified in 18 veterans. Specifically, these included variants in RAD51C , RAD51D , BRCA1 , BRCA2 , PALB2 , MSH6 , and MEN1 in addition to variants in genes that are known founder variants in ATM and MUTYH (Fig 1 and Appendix Table A 1 , online only). One veteran was female and had a PPGV in BRCA1 ; she died within 5 months of this result. Notably, three of these 18 veterans had PPGVs in more than one gene. The primary tumor sites of these 18 veterans included prostate, melanoma, colorectal, lung, cancer of unknown primary, and head and neck cancers. Of note, consistent with inherited risk for prostate cancer, this subpopulation includes PPGV in BRCA2 , PALB2 , and MSH6 . 11 However, 14 veterans had PPGV in tumors not classically associated with cancer predisposition genes. FIG 1. Open in a new tab Veterans with PPGVs by gene, by percentage of total cohort. PPGVs, potentially pathogenic germline variants. These criteria were then applied to the NPOP data set at scale. From a population of 20,014 veterans, 1,200 PPGVs were identified. Assuming at least one PPGV per veteran, this corresponds to a PPGV rate of approximately 6% (as these data were aggregated and fully deidentified, there may be a small subset of veterans who have more than one PPGV). DISCUSSION This study identified PPGV in the veteran population (6%-9%) at a rate similar to inherited risk for cancer in the general population (5%-10%) and as reported in previous studies. 7 , 8 These data point toward the need to expand genetics services for our veteran population, which currently exist only at a handful of centers. 16 Given the increasing utilization of clinical genetics services in cancer care, 11 , 12 tumor-only sequencing results can help identify those patients who would benefit most from germline testing. While we did identify PPGVs that correlate with risk for a veteran''s primary tumor (namely in the prostate cancer subpopulation), the veteran population is particularly enriched in tumor types that otherwise would not warrant referral for genetic counseling, such as lung cancers and carcinoma of unknown primary (CUP). Additionally, a recent report studying a similar population (DoD Cancer Registry) demonstrated that 13.3% of metastatic cancers were of unknown primary, 20 and this diagnosis maybe is over-represented among the older population demographic, as seen in our cohort. 21 As such, our PPGV frequency is dependent on the tumor types that we encounter in our clinic and may be different in a cohort with tumors more traditionally associated with inherited risk. Nevertheless, we have identified a subpopulation of these patients who are appropriate candidates for such a referral on the basis of their tumor-only profiling results, even when their primary tumor is not generally associated with an inherited cancer risk predisposition. Additionally, given the higher prevalence of prostate cancer in this population, these data also reinforce the importance of genetic counseling for this tumor type. A limitation of this study is the lack of confirmatory germline testing of these PPGVs using a matched normal sample (blood or other nontumor sample); we cannot confirm what proportion of PPGVs are truly present in the germline. Also, given that the DNA sequencing was performed at an external clinical laboratory, we are limited to the variants reported on their clinical reports: These external laboratories only reported the amino acid change for a select number of genes with no raw sequencing data available for us to analyze. However, for the purpose of this study, we sought to use the available information to determine how it influenced the ordering provider in recommending further genetic evaluation for patients. For example, the variant allele fraction can aid in the identification of a PPGV in a tumor but is often left off of clinical reports seen by oncologists (conversely, it can be a poor surrogate as it can be very much influenced by tumor sampling and characteristics). 7 , 22 Of note, newer generations of FoundationOne CDx are beginning to include those variants suspicious for a germline origin on the basis of sequencing characteristics. 23 , 24 As tumor-only next-generation sequencing-based platforms are increasingly being used in oncologic care, this study demonstrates the implications of these results beyond informing treatment decisions and really highlighting the need for referral to clinical cancer genetics specialists for detailed family history analysis, genetic counseling, and further genetic testing. Germline confirmation of these PPGVs is an important part of veteran cancer care, 25 informing further cancer surveillance for the veteran and their family members. In this cohort, we show that PPGV findings from tumor-only testing should be incorporated into a standardized workflow of veteran oncologic care, resulting in their referral to cancer genetics counseling. The VA currently faces some challenges to address this increased volume. Only a handful of VA Medical Centers (VAMCs) currently use genetics providers, relying in many cases on referral to community providers (both of which may involve long wait times or long travel distances). In general, genetics providers are concentrated in urban areas, particularly on either coast 26 . Given that 60% of veterans live in rural areas, 16 they are particularly vulnerable to nationwide disparities in provision of these services. Care delivery models are being explored for the provision of genetic services by health care providers who lack formal training in medical genetics; however, they have identified barriers including the time required for adequate consent and explanation of genetic testing, as well as provider comfort with genetic testing technology and result interpretation. 27 , 28 One option is to embed genetics providers within the oncology divisions participating in the NPOP to streamline the referral process from an oncologist to a genetics professional. Virtual care is also a mechanism by which care delivery can occur on a larger scale and capture those veterans without easy access to their VAMC. The Genomic Medicine Service based out of the Salt Lake City VA already successfully uses telehealth to deliver care to over 80 VAMCs and other VA clinics nationwide; the increased reliance upon telehealth by the VA during the COVID-19 pandemic has demonstrated that it can be effectively implemented on even a larger scale, 29 given high veteran satisfaction with these services. 30 As a result of the distance between patient and provider, patient specimens for germline testing could be obtained via saliva testing and sent directly to the testing company; alternately, a veteran could present to their local VA facility for phlebotomy and the blood shipped to the provider''s location for further processing. Additionally, sustainable funding will also be needed to cover the cost of this germline genetic testing, the price of which has decreased dramatically over the years (although still expensive when compared with other clinical laboratory analyses). As many VAMCs only infrequently use genetic testing, individual VA laboratories may face financial difficulties in using it on a regular basis depending on laboratory service agreements. Given this increasing need for genetics services, VA clinical genetics providers could coordinate efforts on a national scale and arrive at the most cost-effective contracts with genetics testing companies to optimize resource utilization by the VA. ACKNOWLEDGMENT The authors thank the Precision Oncology Program at the VA Ann Arbor Healthcare Systems for compiling veteran sequencing data. The authors also thank Dr Sara Ahmed, PhD, of the National Precision Oncology program for the provision of veteran data. APPENDIX TABLE A1. List of Specific PPGV Identified in Veterans Along with Primary Tumor Type Open in a new tab Arathi Mohan Stock and Other Ownership Interests: Johnson and Johnson, Pfizer, Viatris, EI Dupont Michael J. Kelley Research Funding: Novartis (Inst), AstraZeneca (Inst), Bristol Myers Squibb (Inst), Regeneron (Inst), Genentech (Inst) Other Relationship: IBM (Inst) Open Payments Link: https://openpaymentsdata.cms.gov/physician/827136 Nithya Ramnath Research Funding: Merck (Inst), Clovis Oncology (Inst), Clovis Oncology (Inst) No other potential conflicts of interest were reported. SUPPORT Lung Precision Oncology Program (LPOP). Precision Oncology Program Cancer of the Prostate (POPCAP). PI. N.R. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS Administrative support: Shelby Raupp, Brittany Pannecouk, Goutham Narla Collection and assembly of data: Erika Amini, Shelby Raupp, Brittany Pannecouk Data analysis and interpretation: Anthony Scott, Arathi Mohan, Sarah Austin, Michael J. Kelley, Goutham Narla, Nithya Ramnath Manuscript writing: All authors Final approval of manuscript: All authors Accountable for all aspects of the work: All authors AUTHORS'' DISCLOSURES OF POTENTIAL CONFLICTS OF INTEREST Integrating Medical Genetics Into Precision Oncology Practice in the Veterans Health Administration: The Time Is Now The following represents disclosure information provided by authors of this manuscript. All relationships are considered compensated unless otherwise noted. Relationships are self-held unless noted. I = Immediate Family Member, Inst = My Institution. Relationships may not relate to the subject matter of this manuscript. For more information about ASCO''s conflict of interest policy, please refer to www.asco.org/rwc or ascopubs.org/op/authors/author-center . Open Payments is a public database containing information reported by companies about payments made to US-licensed physicians ( Open Payments ). Arathi Mohan Stock and Other Ownership Interests: Johnson and Johnson, Pfizer, Viatris, EI Dupont Michael J. Kelley Research Funding: Novartis (Inst), AstraZeneca (Inst), Bristol Myers Squibb (Inst), Regeneron (Inst), Genentech (Inst) Other Relationship: IBM (Inst) Open Payments Link: https://openpaymentsdata.cms.gov/physician/827136 Nithya Ramnath Research Funding: Merck (Inst), Clovis Oncology (Inst), Clovis Oncology (Inst) No other potential conflicts of interest were reported. REFERENCES 1. Schwaederle M, Zhao M, Lee JJ, et al. Impact of precision medicine in diverse cancers: A meta-analysis of phase II clinical trials J Clin Oncol 333817–38252015 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Chakravarty D, Solit DB.Clinical cancer genomic profiling Nat Rev Genet 22483–5012021 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Meric-Bernstam F, Brusco L, Shaw K, et al. Feasibility of large-scale genomic testing to facilitate enrollment onto genomically matched clinical trials J Clin Oncol 332753–27622015 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Moscow JA, Fojo T, Schilsky RL.The evidence framework for precision cancer medicine Nat Rev Clin Oncol 15183–1922018 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Schrader KA, Cheng DT, Joseph V, et al. Germline variants in targeted tum