INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6207,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12041737','Genetic counselors outside of the genetics clinic: Roles, practices, and ethico-legal implications in light of lagging legal recognition across Canada.','Rojas SK, Adam S; GenCOUNSEL Study; Elliott AM, Zawati MH.','2024','cagc cbgc ethico cagc translatable ethico leeming baty abacan haspel translatable haverbusch hoskovec uhlmann shugar thirlaway ingles machini shugar machini machini shugar biannually cagc cagc cagc cagc waltman scopes dragojlovic patrinos overseen cagc cbgc cbgc cagc cagc cagc cbgc stipulate cbgc abgc cagc cbgc patrinos rhcp rhcp cosigning leeming patrinos zawati leeming shugar unim inglis somers waltman rhcp cagc leeming patrinos zawati patrinos unqualified statute patrinos zawati bensend wrongful wrongful abacan abacan patrinos zawati abacan hoskins shugar ethico ethico rhcps haverbusch cagc cagc cagc infographic gencounsel haverbusch shugar limesurvey balkite baty resta incited rhcp submitter metabolics mbas electrophysiologist fccmg liaising cagc cagc cagc cagc cagc cagc shipped requisitions cagc cagc ferrier cagc cagc cagc londre flagging gcas dragojlovic pirzadeh cagc gcas ooc salm vassy waitlists hynes provisioning bowdin hoskovec ethico shugar unim hynes unim unim rhcps hercher rhcps phrasing rhcp cagc cbgc patrinos unwittingly unqualified unqualified bensend rhcp rhcp patrinos rhcp cagc cbgc rhcps rhcp patrinos unvalidated cagc gencounsel unvalidated clarifications rhcps ethico cagc cbgc incongruent translatable waitlists samantha zawati shelin samantha shelin zawati gencounsel gencounsel jehanine bartha larry lynd alivia shelin nick bansback lorne nick dragojlovic debby daryl pullman virani zawati gencounsel lsarp zawati frqs zawati ethico e1943 gencounsel zawati zawati gencounsel jehanine bartha larry lynd alivia nick bansback lorne nick dragojlovic debby daryl pullman virani abacan alsubaie caanen davoine elackatt malmgren kejriwal lantigua lodahl wicklund mcnally baty kullo madueme salberg e000054 balkite uhlmann yashar baty els a0005615 bensend veach niendorf bowdin bedoukian carew bjornsson blitzer deardorff demmer hindorff hisama krantz hoskovec leonhard cagc cagc accg cagc 20practice 20based 20competencies cagc cagc accg cagc 20knowledge 20based 20competencies 20june allanson permaul telner blumenschein lilley lilley hume somerville thirlaway s00439 bdr2 dragojlovic souich karnebeek lynd ejmg dragojlovic borle kopac nisselle lynd dragojlovic lynd ferrier grewal robb rutberg lilley cagc secord qagc cagc accg 20professional 20order 20genetic 20counsellors 20in 20quebec 20may 202016_finaleng geneticalliance langlois lynd haspel genzen haverbusch heise callanan hercher pondering hoskins gaff macciocca pearn shalhoub salvemini milward hoskovec davanzo wicklund hynes predham etchegary s13053 ingles yeates semsarian hrthm inglis koehn mcgillivray sunrise patrinos zawati leeming demers steinecke bourgeault hcpol londre zaleski conta machini braxton tsipis taverner moreton houghton melville agnc gcrb ahcs krautscheid tvrdik openshaw lagrave 164a linkclick fileticket yxsx6xw2hgm portalid cagc accg 20regulation 20cases 20harm cagc cagc accg 20canadian 20pss 20full 20report 20oct laurino wessels macaulay patrinos gencounsel zawati pirzadeh resta 142c procop kottke wyllie lacbawan jmoldx dowell replicability salm abbate ottman marder alcalay leech taber klitzman shugar quercia trevors rabideau somers jefferies kubendran uhlmann hoskovec freivogel unim vito villari zawati vassy 287fs219 aaa2401 mianzo nagaraj tawde uhlmann yashar waltman runke balcom lilley zetzsche goodenberger zawati conseillers genetique medicales infirmieres brouillees droit mcgil zawati chapitre responsabilite civile conseiller genetique forcier savard droit politiques lexis nexis','Abstract Advances in medical genetics have led to a significant increase in demand for genetic services and expertise across almost all medical specialties. Genetic counselors (GCs) in Canada play key roles in genetic services both within and outside of the Genetics Clinic, while not being regulated or legally recognized as healthcare professionals (HCPs) in most provinces. Understanding whether GCs outside of the “traditional” Genetics Clinic influence patient care, their level of professional autonomy and supervisory structure is, therefore, important. In this study, we explore the current landscape of GC practice outside of the Genetics Clinic by describing positions, determining the professional scope of practice, as defined by the Canadian Association of Genetic Counselors (CAGC) and Canadian Board of Genetic Counseling (CBGC) core competencies, and by elucidating associated ethico‐legal implications. An online survey was developed and distributed to GCs working with patient‐related data in Canada in positions outside of the Genetics Clinic through the CAGC ListServ and accessed between March 5 and April 9, 2021. Thirty GCs were included in the study, with 16/30 in public healthcare system positions. Most respondents held roles with direct (11/30) and indirect (14/30) impact on patient care and management, and the majority reported performing their primary roles with minimal supervision (56%) or complete independence (36%). Most roles (22/25) elicited by respondents were considered to be within the GC scope of practice, except for administrative tasks and special projects. GCs were the only genetics‐trained professional(s) in 8/30 of respondents'' workplaces. The results of the current study support the value of GCs translatable skillset in positions beyond the Genetics Clinic, and outline ethico‐legal implications for GCs, regulated HCPs, patients, and health institutions in the absence of legal recognition, including medical‐legal liability and title protection. This study provides evidence in support of regulation of GCs as HCPs. Keywords: genetic counselors, legal recognition, policy, roles What is known about the topic Genetic counselors are positioned in a variety of settings outside of the Genetics Clinic. In some instances, genetic counselors are the only genetics‐trained health professionals in the workplace, while not being legally recognized as healthcare professionals in most Canadian provinces. What this paper adds to the topic Genetic counselors outside of the Genetics Clinic largely work within their scope of practice with high levels of autonomy, providing genetics expertise and influencing patient care. The results of this study provide evidence in support of legal recognition and regulation of genetic counselors as healthcare professionals in Canada. 1. INTRODUCTION 1.1. Expanding opportunities in genetic counseling practice The profession of genetic counseling first emerged within a health institution''s Department or Division of Medical Genetics (hereby referred to as the Genetics Clinic for simplicity) (Leeming, 2013 ), and is considered the traditional setting for genetic counselors (GCs). The roles and practices of GCs in this context have been well described in the literature (Baty, 2016 ; NSGC, 2020 ). Advances in genetic knowledge and genomic technology in recent years have led to a surge in demand for genetic services across almost all medical specialties in both public and private practice (Abacan et al., 2019 ). Despite this increase, most non‐genetics trained healthcare professionals (HCPs) have limited knowledge and inadequate training to make use of these advances appropriately (Haspel et al., 2021 ; Radford et al., 2014 ). This gap, and a highly translatable skill set, poised GCs to begin practicing across medical specialties outside of the Genetics Clinic (Haverbusch et al., 2019 ). GCs are also positioned outside of the clinical setting, including laboratories, research, education, public health, and policy (Hoskovec et al., 2018 ; Uhlmann et al., 2020 ). 1.1.1. Clinical Genetic counselors outside of the genetics clinic Clinical (i.e., patient‐facing) GCs positioned outside the Genetics Clinic are presumed to continue performing their primary roles (Cohen, 2020 ) while also acting as the genetics experts in their clinical setting (Shugar et al., 2017 ). It has been proposed that these GCs also take on additional roles with greater autonomy. Such roles may include training and providing expert consultation and guidance to non‐genetics HCPs (Carroll et al., 2019 ; Clarke & Thirlaway, 2011 ; Ingles et al., 2011 ; Machini et al., 2014 ; Radford et al., 2014 ; Shugar et al., 2017 ), clinical decision‐making with regard to provision of genetic testing (i.e., decision to offer testing) (Machini et al., 2014 ), guiding test selection (Ahmad et al., 2019 ), and interpreting and reporting genetic test results (Machini et al., 2014 ; Shugar et al., 2017 ). 1.1.2. Non‐clinical genetic counselors As GCs possess skills that translate to a variety of work settings, including the ability to tailor, translate, and communicate complex information in a simple, relevant way for a broad range of audiences, strong interpersonal skills, research skills, and in‐depth knowledge of healthcare delivery (National Society of Genetic Counselors, 2009 ), they have also begun to take on non‐clinical positions. The Professional Status Survey (PSS) provides data on the genetic counseling profession. It is administered by the NSGC on an annual basis to its members, and biannually to CAGC members in collaboration with the CAGC. Given our focus on the Canadian landscape and due to the timing of our study, data from Professional Status Survey 2020: Canada (2020) best characterizes our study population. According to the 2020 PSS (NSGC & CAGC, 2020 ), 15% of Canadian respondents work in non‐direct patient care roles, with an additional 25% working in mixed (direct and non‐direct patient care) roles. Non‐clinical GCs in Canada are mostly positioned in diagnostic laboratories (public and commercial), universities, and academic medical centers (NSGC & CAGC, 2020 ). Laboratory‐based GCs act as a customer liaison, interpret and report results, take on roles in sales and marketing, research, management duties, test development and performance, and education (Christian et al., 2012 ; Waltman et al., 2016 ). Less is described regarding non‐clinical GCs in other settings, such as research. The roles and responsibilities of non‐clinical GCs in such positions are presumably variable, considering they may or may not involve direct patient interaction or the use of patient‐related data (Burns et al., 2019 ; Cohen, 2020 ). A recent scoping review by our group on the composition and capacity of the clinical genetic workforce revealed that there is overlap in responsibilities of providers, a shortage of genetics providers, and lack of consensus about the appropriate boundaries between the scopes of practice for genetics and non‐genetics providers (Dragojlovic et al., 2020 ). 1.2. The state of legal recognition of genetic counselors in Canada Canadian GCs are not regulated nor legally recognized as HCPs in most provinces, meaning there is no legal requirement to become certified (Patrinos et al., 2020 ). Rather, GCs are informally overseen by the CAGC and the newly created certification body, the Canadian Board of Genetic Counseling (CBGC; Lambert et al., 2021 ). The CBGC ensures competency of GCs through establishment of knowledge‐ and practice‐based competencies required for certification (CAGC, 2012 ; CAGC Certification Board, 2018 ). The scope of practice and qualifications of GCs in Canada are, therefore, determined by the CAGC and CBGC, with no means of enforcement in the absence of provincial regulation. While not required by law, most Canadian healthcare institutions that hire GCs will stipulate that candidates be Board eligible or Board certified with the CBGC or have active candidate status with the American Board of Genetic Counseling (ABGC), although this may not necessarily be stipulated by employers hiring GCs for non‐clinical roles (Lambert et al., 2021 ). It is, therefore, possible for individuals to use the title of GC without the required training or competencies outlined by the CAGC and CBGC (Patrinos et al., 2020 ). In Canada, GCs typically work as part of a healthcare team, under the supervision of a regulated HCP (RHCP). When a GC is involved in a patient''s care, the supervising RHCP is ultimately responsible for medical acts such as prescribing genetic testing and cosigning medical documentation as it relates to diagnosis and medical management recommendations (Leeming, 2013 ; Patrinos et al., 2020 ; Zawati, 2012 ). Presently and historically, medical geneticists (MG) often provide this clinical oversight to GCs within the Genetics Clinic (Leeming, 2013 ; Shugar et al., 2017 ; Unim et al., 2020 ). While the organizational structure and workflow of clinical GCs in non‐Genetics services and laboratory‐based GCs has been outlined in some articles (Ahmad et al., 2019 ; Christian et al., 2012 ; Inglis et al., 2015 ; Somers et al., 2014 ; Waltman et al., 2016 ), the level of supervision and delegation practices in these settings remain largely unknown. Less information is available for GCs in other non‐clinical roles and non‐hospital settings, and for all, practices described in the literature are unlikely to be consistent across institutions. It can be deduced from the 2020 PSS that a MG is not always the supervising RHCP for Canadian GCs working outside of the Genetics Clinic (NSGC & CAGC, 2020 ). An important consideration to the expansion of genetic services to other specialties through implementation of GCs within these services is how a genetic test can be ordered from a legal standpoint. Despite having the relevant training and expertise, GCs across Canada are legally unable to order genetic testing. Ordering of a genetic test is a protected medical act (Leeming, 2013 ; Patrinos et al., 2020 ; Zawati, 2012 ), meaning that physicians are the sole HCPs who can prescribe a genetic test for a patient, unless otherwise permitted by law (e.g., delegation). For example, the medical act of communicating a diagnosis relating to a genetic disease or disorder has been delegated to GCs in Manitoba; which is the first, and to date, the only province in Canada to legally recognize GCs by doing so (Patrinos et al., 2020 ). Another mechanism for legal recognition of HCPs is through regulation. The overarching purpose of regulating healthcare professions is to protect the public from unqualified individuals providing health services, by regulating the practice of professionals and protecting their titles (Leslie et al., 2022 ). Similar to the United States (US), in Canada, healthcare workers are regulated at the provincial level (vs. state level in the US), often by the Minister of Health. Every province has its own framework and requirements for the regulation of HCPs. Professions are most commonly regulated through umbrella legislation (e.g., the Health Professions Act [HPA]) in which multiple health professions are covered by one statute or, alternatively, through profession‐specific statutes (Lambert et al., 2021 ; Patrinos et al., 2020 ; Zawati, 2012 , 2018 ). HCP regulation is warranted when there is risk for harm to the public. In the genetic counseling context, specific examples of harm have been cited in support of legislation in both the US and Canada (Bensend et al., 2014 ; Fox et al., 2016 ; Kelley, & Office of Policy, Research, and Regulatory Reform, 2013 ; NSGC, 2016 ) and grouped into the following categories: incomplete risk assessment, inaccurate test interpretation, psychological and financial repercussions, inadequate training, inadequate training specialized in genetics, title misuse, medical malpractice, and alleged wrongful birth or wrongful termination of pregnancy (Kelley, & Office of Policy, Research, and Regulatory Reform, 2013 ). As the profession continues to expand internationally, GCs find themselves at various stages of the professional recognition continuum even within the same country. Relative to countries with professional regulation through avenues such as licensure, including the US, United Kingdom (UK), Israel, and Saudi Arabia (Abacan et al., 2019 ; Middleton et al., 2023 ; NSGC, 2021 ) legal recognition of GCs as HCPs in Canada is lagging — GCs are not regulated, nor do they benefit from title protection in any Canadian province (Abacan et al., 2019 ; Ormond et al., 2018 ; Patrinos et al., 2020 ; Zawati, 2018 ). There are some countries in which there exists a professional registry, such as the UK, the European Union (Middleton et al., 2023 ), and South Africa; while others, including Japan, Taiwan, and Australia, rely on self‐regulation as is done in Canada (Abacan et al., 2019 ). Interestingly, the self‐regulation of GCs in Australia is formalized through the National Alliance of Self‐Regulating Health Professionals (Hoskins et al., 2021 ). Shugar et al. were the first to systematically evaluate the scope of practice and potential risk for harm associated with genetic counseling practice in Ontario, Canada (2017). While they identified risk for harm in genetic counseling practice, they concluded the potential risk for harm is greater than the realized risk. To our knowledge, no study has attempted to explore the Canadian landscape and practices of GCs in various positions outside of the Genetics Clinic through an ethico‐legal lens. 2. STUDY OBJECTIVES The current landscape of genetic counseling in positions outside of the Genetics Clinic in Canada remains unknown, especially considering lagging legal recognition across the country. The shift beyond the “traditional” GC role and setting has brought on new and expanded roles and responsibilities. Additionally, the use of patient‐related data (i.e., health‐related information such as medical history, current health and/or diagnoses, test results, therapies, lifestyle choices, genetic, and genomic data [Canadian Institute for Health Information, 2022 ; Genetic Alliance UK, 2016 ]) and the presence of other genetics‐trained professionals in the workplace must also be considered. In the absence of legal recognition of GCs as HCPs, it is important to understand (1) whether the work of GCs in these contexts is influencing patient care, (2) their level of professional autonomy, and (3) their supervisory structure. These three facets, which have not been previously explored in the Canadian context, could have important ethico‐legal implications: namely, not only the risk of harm to patients or the public but also legal liability for GCs, RHCPs, and the Canadian public healthcare system. Without a complete understanding of the organizational structure and the use of patient‐related data, these implications remain unknown. This exploratory study aims to describe the current practices of Canadian GCs in positions outside of the Genetics Clinic working with patient‐related data by: Describing Canadian genetic counseling positions outside of the Genetics Clinic. Assessing whether roles and workflows of GCs within these positions are within the professional scope of practice. Elucidating potential implications of GCs work with patient‐related data in the ethical/legal context. 3. METHODS 3.1. Study design In this exploratory study, a mixed methods approach was selected to capture baseline demographic data and descriptions of genetic counseling positions outside of the Genetics Clinic across Canada. An online survey was administered nationally to maximize response rate, given the small estimated size of the target population. Ethical approval for the study was obtained by the McGill University Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences. 3.2. Target population and recruitment The study was designed to capture a broad variety of responses and was, therefore, not expected to be generalizable or reach data saturation. Eligible participants satisfied the three following criteria: (1) GC working in Canada, (2) employed in positions partially or completely based outside of a Genetics Clinic, and (3) working with patient‐related data. With limited data, we estimated that 9–15 GCs from major Canadian cities alone (including Vancouver, Toronto, and Montreal) would be eligible to participate in this study. Given the small number, we chose to recruit GCs from across Canada to maximize the number of responses, despite differing HCP regulation at the provincial level. GCs in positions outside of the Genetics Clinic may still be affiliated with a Genetics Clinic (Haverbusch et al., 2019 ), so GCs in positions partially based within a Genetics Clinic were also included to capture all possible positions outside of the traditional genetics setting. Finally, given the context of legal recognition, we focused on GCs working with patient‐related data, assuming these positions were most likely to have an impact on patient care, and, therefore, a potential risk for patient harm. An email with a description of the study and link to the online survey (including online study consent form) was sent to full members of the CAGC via the CAGC ListServ and the CAGC Non‐clinical and Research Communities of Practice. Additionally, an infographic with a brief description and study link, was shared via the GenCOUNSEL project on social media (Twitter, Facebook, and Instagram). The survey was accessed between March 5 and April 9, 2021. 3.3. Procedures and data collection The survey was designed by the first author in consultation with GCs and researchers from the study team. A 24‐item unique survey was developed consisting of both open (6) and close‐ended questions (18) (see Appendix S1 ), based on the aims of the study and guided by topics that emerged from a literature review (Haverbusch et al., 2019 ; Shugar et al., 2017 ). The survey was housed in the LimeSurvey online program licensed by McGill University and participation was anonymous. Participants were asked to describe their five principal roles and to rate their level of professional autonomy in each role. Questions to ascertain demographic information, position details, and perceived influence on patient management were also included. 3.4. Qualitative data analysis An inductive approach to qualitative data analysis was used for open‐ended responses regarding supervisor title and perceived impact on patient management (Braun & Clarke, 2021 ). Open text responses were independently coded by the first and second author to establish inter‐rater reliability. Text data were assigned codes, which were collated into emerging categories. The codes and categories were then jointly reviewed, consolidated, named, and defined by the first and second authors. A deductive approach was utilized for the analysis of the open‐ended responses describing respondents'' principal roles (Roberts et al., 2019 ). Based on literature review of GC roles, it was anticipated that responses would fall into the following categories: clinical, education, research, laboratory, industry, and policy (Balkite & Smith, 2009 ; Baty, 2016 ; Fraser, 1974 ; Resta, 2006 ; Walker, 2009 ). First, open text responses were independently coded by the first and second authors using an inductive approach (Braun & Clarke, 2021 ). Open‐ended questions incited variable responses and some were coded for multiple themes. Responses congruent with multiple themes were coded accordingly, meaning the sum of responses per theme is not representative of the total number of responses. The codes were then jointly reviewed by the first and second authors, discrepancies were discussed and consolidated. The first and second authors then classified the coded themes into the above‐mentioned categories, which, following further discussion were consolidated and renamed. 3.5. Quantitative data analysis Descriptive statistics were utilized to analyze demographic information and responses to close‐ended questions. To achieve a 95% confidence level at a confidence interval of 5, a minimum sample size of 93 would be required to allow for statistical generalization. Further analysis for statistical significance was, therefore, not possible as the sample size did not meet requirements for statistical power. Results of the thematic analysis of respondents'' five principal roles were quantified by describing the number of responses coded to each subcategory. Duplicates (i.e., one respondent citing more than one role classified into the same subcategory) were removed in order to represent the number of respondents performing the same type of role. Respondents were asked to describe their level of professional autonomy. Each question related to the previously listed principal roles, had a 4‐point Likert‐style response indicating the extent to which they received supervision by a RHCP. Possible responses ranged from maximum, moderate, and minimal supervision, and complete independence. Responses to the five questions from all respondents were combined and quantified in Microsoft Excel. 4. RESULTS Seventy‐one respondents accessed the online survey, of whom 52 provided consent to participate in the study. After prompting for eligibility criteria, 10 were ineligible and did not continue with the survey. Of the 42 respondents who participated in the survey, 10 responses were incomplete, and two were found to be duplicate entries from a single submitter. The responses within the duplicate entries were inconsistent and, therefore, excluded from analysis. This led to a total of 30 participants. While it is typically appropriate to estimate the participation rates as compared to the results of the PSS most relevant to the period of data collection, the 2020 PSS data does not contain the detailed information that would allow us to determine the number of GCs meeting our study criteria. Consequently, it is not possible to accurately estimate the size of the eligible population and thus determine the response rate. 4.1. Participant demographics and GC positions Basic demographic information and a description of respondents'' positions by professional role and work setting are summarized in Table 1 . The largest proportion of respondents were in Ontario (14/30) followed by British Columbia (5/30) and Quebec (5/30). The proportion of responses per province is in keeping with the distribution of GCs across Canadian provinces, as estimated by Lambert et al. ( 2021 ). Most (17/30) graduated between 2010 and 2019. All but one respondent reported a single work setting, which allowed for the reporting of professional roles by work setting, as described in Figure 1 . “Other” settings included publicly funded clinical diagnostic laboratories (3 respondents), a public hospital (1 respondent), a genetics research laboratory (1 respondent), and a Precision Child Health Initiative (1 respondent). Reported fields of practice included cancer (8 respondents), molecular laboratory (7 respondents), research (6 respondents), cardiovascular (5 respondents), general genetics (2 respondents), industry (2 respondents), newborn screening (1 respondent), infertility/ART (1 respondent), primary care (1 respondent), and metabolics (1 respondent). One respondent did not provide a response for the field of practice. TABLE 1. Demographic and workplace descriptors of respondents. N = 30 Province British Columbia 5 Alberta 3 Manitoba 2 Ontario 14 Quebec 5 Nova‐Scotia 1 Year of graduation 2010–2019 17 2000–2009 7 1999 and earlier 6 PROFESSIONAL role Clinical 13 Laboratory 12 Research 12 Academic/education 4 Other 2 WORK setting Non‐genetics clinic (Public sector) 13 Industry 7 Private pay clinic 2 University 3 Government 0 Other 6 Open in a new tab FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Respondents'' professional roles by work setting ( n = 30). Participants could select more than one item to describe their role. 4.2. Clinical structure and supervision Respondents'' supervisors were coded as specialist MDs (who were not clinical geneticists; 7 respondents), medical/clinical geneticists (MD; 6 respondents), administrators/MBAs (Masters of Business Administration; 5 respondent), molecular geneticists (PhD; 4 respondents), GCs (4 respondents), PhD Scientists (3 respondents), genomics technical managers (2 respondents), and GCs with a PhD (2 respondents). Four respondents cited two supervisors with different titles and one respondent did not provide a response ( n = 34). Specialist MDs included a cardiologist, cardiac electrophysiologist, reproductive endocrinologist, pediatrician, and pediatric hematologist–oncologist. Respondents were also asked whether they were the only genetics‐trained professional in their workplace (Figure 2 ). Five answered “yes,” 23 answered “no,” and two did not respond ( n = 30). Those who responded “no” were asked to specify the qualifications of other genetics‐trained professionals in the workplace and could select more than one descriptor. Nineteen respondents selected “GC,” 14 selected “PhD,” 12 selected “MD,” and 3 selected “other” ( n = 23). “Other” responses were “MSc” (two respondents) and FCCMG (one respondent). Of note, 3/23 respondents only selected “GC” to describe the other genetics‐trained professionals in the workplace. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Qualifications of other genetics‐trained professionals in the workplace ( n = 30). Participants could select more than one item. ‘N/A’ describes respondents who indicated being the sole genetics‐trained professional in their work setting. 4.3. Principal roles Respondents were asked to describe their five principal roles in open text and to list any additional roles involving patient‐related data. Analysis of the open text responses pertaining to specific roles and responsibilities yielded a variety of codes. These were organized into the following categories: clinical, laboratory, business, research, education, and operations, as described in Figure 3 . FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Frequency of coded themes by category, following thematic analysis of primary roles and responsibilities listed by participants ( n = 30). Some responses were coded to multiple themes, therefore, the sum of references per theme is not representative of the total number of roles indicated by participants. 4.3.1. Category 1: Clinical 23/30 respondents described roles that were categorized into the clinical category. The most commonly coded role described by participants was that of direct patient care (19/30). Some indicated typical clinical genetic counseling roles, as illustrated by Respondent 18 (Clinical GC in a Private‐pay clinic): “Pre‐test counseling on carrier screening, PGT [pre‐implantation genetic testing], karyotype, etc.” Others related to non‐clinical roles: “Returning genomic sequencing results to research participants” (Respondent 22, Research GC). The second most common theme generated within the clinical category was indirect patient care (9/30), such as “Specialty genetic testing send out coordination, funding review, submission” (Respondent 8, Clinical GC in a Non‐Genetics clinic). 4.3.2. Category 2: Laboratory 16/30 respondents described roles that were categorized into the laboratory category. Roles described by respondents were either coded to lab operations (8/30) or variant assessment (9/30). Review of lab accounting, paying bills, etc. Respondent 70 (Laboratory GC) Interpretation of the research data to identify actionable secondary findings. Respondent 71 (Mixed Laboratory/Clinical GC) 4.3.3. Category 3: Business 9/30 respondents described roles that were categorized into the business category, with the most common theme being customer service roles (8/30). “General liaising with other professionals, responding to inquiries, etc.” (Respondent 25, Mixed Clinical/Research GC). 4.3.4. Category 4: Research 14/30 respondents described roles that were categorized into the research category, the majority of which were coded as “research activities” (9/30). “Data analysis/manuscript writing” (Respondent 61, Laboratory GC in Industry). 4.3.5. Category 5: Education 20/30 respondents described roles that were categorized into the education category. The most common themes within this category were roles related to teaching students (8/30) and knowledge dissemination (8/30). Knowledge dissemination (e.g., Presentations/updates on genetic counseling service and manuscript preparation). Respondent 24 (Mixed Clinical/Research GC) Educator of residents and genetic counseling students on exome analysis. Respondent 29 (Laboratory GC in Industry) 4.3.6. Category 6: Operations 23/30 respondents described roles that could not be organized into one of the pre‐existing categories. The six generated themes were classified into the new category of operations. The most commonly recurring themes within this category were roles coded as administrative tasks (15/30) and clinic/research coordination (9/30). Administrative work for [patient] access to genetic services. Respondent 32 (Research GC in Industry) Clinic management and coordination. Respondent 46 (Mixed Clinical/Laboratory GC) [Coordination] of a research study. Respondent 38 (Mixed Clinical/Laboratory GC in Industry) 4.4. Scope of practice Coded roles from respondents were matched to the CAGC Core Competency to which they correspond, as described in the CAGC Knowledge‐based and Practice‐based competencies documents (CAGC, 2012 ; CAGC Certification Board, 2018 ) (summarized in Table S2 of Appendix S1 ). Of the 25 coded principal roles described by study respondents, 19 corresponded to a CAGC Core Competency, while six did not. The principal roles that did not correspond to a CAGC Core Competency were genetic testing, lab operations, sales, marketing, administrative tasks, and special projects. 4.5. Professional autonomy Respondents were asked to assign the level of supervision they receive while performing each of the principal roles they had listed using a Likert‐style four‐point scale. Each respondent could, therefore, provide five responses ( n = 150). Most roles (84; 56%) were performed with minimal supervision, followed by complete independence (54; 36%) and moderate supervision (12; 8%). No respondents reported roles performed with maximal supervision. 4.6. Perceived impact on patient care and management Respondents were asked whether their work influenced patient care and management, and to elaborate in an open text response. Themes that emerged from analysis of the responses were coded as indirect impact (14/30), direct impact (11/30), no impact (1/30), and no response (4/30). 4.6.1. Theme 1: Indirect impact Many respondents described instances in which their work had the potential to influence patient care and management, although not directly. A response was coded as “indirect impact” when a different healthcare provider was making a decision (e.g., a diagnosis or test selection), based on or informed by a GCs work. The results of WES/WGS as interpreted and reported by me (with or without input from/review by a second analyst, depending on the case) have the potential to influence patient care and management once reported to the referring clinician. Respondent 28 (Research GC) 4.6.2. Theme 2: Direct impact Some respondents reported their role as having a direct impact on patient care and management. The descriptions involved impact at various stages of the patient care pathway including test selection, results interpretation, psychosocial counseling, and cascade evaluation of relatives. […] In my role I work in the capacity of a traditional [GC] – providing pre‐ and post‐test counseling to patients and their families for the purposes of informing their care and management for their inherited cardiac conditions, educating them about their condition and complex genetic concepts, and providing psychosocial assessment of their needs as they [relate] to their inherited condition. The only difference is that I do this outside of a genetics clinic without regular involvement with a geneticist. Respondent 13 (Laboratory GC) […] I choose appropriate testing and coordinate all genetic testing […]. I decide when samples are shipped, how urgently they must be prepared, and how urgently results must be delivered. I fill out all testing requisitions and ensure that adequate clinical information has been provided. Respondent 27 (Laboratory GC) 5. DISCUSSION 5.1. GC positions outside of the genetics clinic Thirty GCs across Canada completed an anonymous online survey designed to obtain baseline information regarding positions, roles, responsibilities, and supervisory structure for GCs working with patient‐related data outside of the Genetics Clinic. Study respondents represented a broad variety of professional GC roles comprised of clinical, laboratory, research, and industry positions, many of which were hybrid. Respondents represented positions in the public and private sectors (for both clinics and diagnostic laboratories), as well as academic and non‐academic healthcare institutions. Many (17/30) respondents were positioned within the public health system (either in non‐genetics departments/divisions or genetic diagnostic laboratories). This is likely due to the healthcare system in Canada being largely public with fewer private/industry positions, and is consistent with the 2020 Canadian PSS results (NSGC & CAGC, 2020 ). Respondents reported various areas of practice, often citing more than one field of practice within their own role, with cancer, molecular laboratory, and research being listed most frequently. Surprisingly, neurogenetics, which is a common and well‐established subspecialty field of practice in genetic counseling, was not listed by study participants. This could be due to such GCs not being captured in our study. 5.2. Principal roles of GCs and clinical implications 5.2.1. Scope of practice In 2012, the CAGC adopted knowledge‐based and practice‐based competencies, together termed Core Competencies, to establish basic requirements for Canadian GCs to gain entry into the profession and provide professional services (Ferrier et al., 2013 ). These statements are meant to outline competencies possessed by all GCs that can be applied, when necessary, regardless of the designated role, setting, client population, or practice environment. In the context of the current study, these Core Competencies were considered the Canadian scope of practice and were, therefore, used to determine whether GC practice outside of the Genetics Clinic, as described by study participants, fell within scope. 19/25 coded principal roles corresponded to a Core Competency. Out of the 6 roles that did not directly correspond to a core competency, 4 could be attributed to certain skills listed within the Core Competencies. These roles were lab operations, sales, marketing, and “genetic testing.” For example, while GCs are not traditionally trained in marketing, they are able to “ Convey information to clients in a manner that meets their needs and levels of understanding ” (Practice‐based Competency 1.5.3) and “ Provide clear […] written information which is tailored to the needs of the clients and collaborators and reflective of the services ” (Practice‐based Competency 1.5.5) (CAGC, 2012 ). With regard to “genetic testing,” roles pertaining to the provision and selection of genetic testing were grouped together during qualitative analysis. As per Practice‐based competencies 1.5.4 and 2.2.2 (CAGC, 2012 ), GCs have the necessary skills to discuss available genetic testing options. While GCs are legally unable to order genetic testing themselves in Canada, they “ have the necessary knowledge, skills, attitude and judgement to […] synthesize relevant information as the basis for assessing […] testing options […] in conjunction with collaborator ” (CAGC, 2012 ) and can, therefore, inform genetic testing selection and provision. Not only does genetic test provision fall within the scope of genetic counseling but the involvement of GCs in this process has also demonstrated substantial cost benefits, and improves efficiency by reducing the number of inappropriate tests ordered (Cook et al., 2021 ; Londre et al., 2017 ; Miller et al., 2014 ; Riley et al., 2015 ; Suarez et al., 2017 ; Wakefield et al., 2018 ). The roles of ‘administrative tasks’ and ‘special projects’ (2/6) did not correspond to any of the Core Competencies. ‘Administrative tasks’ was the second most cited role (15/30). Such administrative tasks included scheduling, emails, sample coordination, flagging patient charts, making referrals, and booking patient appointments. While practically inevitable, ‘administrative tasks’ are hardly considered a core competency for GCs. In fact, many Genetics Clinics have implemented Genetic Counseling Assistants/Genetic Assistants (GCAs/GAs) to reduce the administrative burden on GCs, and so GCs can spend more time on ‘top of scope’ roles (Dragojlovic et al., 2020 ; Pirzadeh‐Miller et al., 2017 ). According to the 2020 PSS, approximately 1/3 of all Canadian respondents, including 24% of GCs in non‐direct patient care positions, reported having access to a GCA (NSGC & CAGC, 2020 ). Do GCs in positions outside of the Genetics Clinic face higher loads of administrative tasks compared to Genetics Clinic positions? It is possible that our results (i.e., 15/30 respondents citing administrative tasks as one of their principal roles) reflect a lack of support staff for GCs in these workplaces, although this question cannot not be adequately addressed by our study. Our study has demonstrated, however, that in some situations, GCs may be the only genetics‐trained professional in the workplace, which could potentially present a barrier to obtaining additional support staff, such as GCAs, to relieve the burden of administrative tasks. These results demonstrate that GCs outside of the Genetics Clinic are taking on new roles and responsibilities, the majority of which are within the Canadian GC scope of practice. While some of these roles do not obviously fall into specific competencies within the Canadian GC scope of practice, GCs clearly exhibit a unique skillset that can be applied and further developed in these settings. 5.2.2. Genetic testing provision and selection In the Canadian public healthcare system, many genetic tests are not available within a given province and must be sent to external diagnostic laboratories in different provinces or out‐of‐country. With limited funding, prior approval for provincial funding must be obtained for out‐of‐province (OOP) or out‐of‐country (OOC) genetic testing. While each province has different processes, the common considerations across systems are clinical utility and cost (Christian et al., 2015 ). Applying for provincial funding, therefore, carries a risk of being denied if results do not have an impact on a patient''s clinical management (Grant et al., 2021 ). Non‐genetics physicians are generally not comfortable with the ordering, coordination, and interpretation of genetic tests (Carroll et al., 2019 ; Salm et al., 2014 ; Vassy et al., 2015 ). Cook et al.''s study found that GCs aiding with test selection provide more timely access to genetics services and are a more cost‐effective alternative compared to the implementation of other genetics‐trained professionals (MDs/PhDs) (2021). Considering the shortage of genetics‐trained professionals and excessively long waitlists for evaluation in Canadian Genetics Clinics (Hynes et al., 2020 ), alternative models of genetic service provision must be seriously considered both within and external to Genetics Clinics. Within the current study, most respondents (24/30) were reportedly involved in the ordering of genetic testing. Such involvement included informing or provisioning of genetic testing for patients, as well as test selection, and the interpretation of results. Those who reported genetic testing (e.g., provision of genetic testing and test selection) as a principal role ( n = 5) performed these roles with minimal supervision (2/5) or complete independence (3/5). Cook et al found physician specialty to be the strongest predictor for whether a physician followed the recommended genomic testing strategy (2021). GCs positioned within other services provide an opportunity to address this concern by building relationships with non‐genetics physicians within specific specialties, thereby facilitating access to and increasing the likelihood of the most appropriate test being ordered. Furthermore, working closely with non‐genetics physician specialists can build a GCs expertise within a specific specialty, aiding with results interpretation. Notably, our study demonstrates that some GCs are autonomously deciding on the provision of genetic testing and test selection for patients. According to international guidelines, an ordering clinician should be able to perform a basic clinical genetics evaluation, including an evaluation of prior genetic tests, be able to determine the best test based on the specific clinical indication, and provide adequate input from genetics professional (Bowdin et al., 2016 ). GCs have the specialized training and skills to perform such an evaluation and increase access to genetic testing and services (Hoskovec et al., 2018 ; Stoll et al., 2018 ). 5.3. Ethico‐legal implications Our results indicate that GCs working with patient‐related data outside of the Genetics Clinic can have an impact on patient care, regardless of setting (clinical vs. non‐clinical), and work with high levels of professional autonomy. These results are consistent with Shugar et al.''s study of clinical GCs in Ontario, in which they concluded that GC practice in Ontario, Canada meets the primary criteria to be considered for regulation (2017). To our knowledge, ours is the first study to describe professional autonomy of Canadian GCs working in positions outside of the Genetics Clinic. Many barriers to genetic services exist in Canada (Unim et al., 2020 ) resulting in, at times, year(s) long wait times for consultation in a Genetics Clinic (Hynes et al., 2020 ). Within the established MG‐led model for the provision of genetic services (Unim et al., 2020 ) and with GCs being unable to order genetic testing autonomously, there is a dependence on a limited number of MGs within the country to access genetic services. This model of genetics service delivery is not sustainable within the current healthcare system (Unim et al., 2020 ), and needs to be addressed as demand continues to grow. GCs occupying positions outside of the Genetics Clinic, to support non‐genetics RHCPs in the provision of genetic testing and results interpretation, can increase access to high‐quality genetic services. Within this model, however, an issue remains: provision of genetic services depends on non‐genetics regulated HCPs who may lack adequate training and competencies in clinical genetics; and patient access to GCs remains limited. While in Canada we are dealing with a public healthcare system, the situation regarding access to genetic counseling is comparable to that in the US with Bill H.R. 3235 (Hercher, 2021 ) and more broadly comparable to other countries seeking professional recognition and regulation in spite of a low national number of GCs. The results of our study support that GCs facilitate access to quality genetic services outside of the Genetics Clinic and indicate that they are doing so with high levels of professional autonomy. GCs are best suited, both in training and cost to the healthcare system (Cook et al., 2021 ), to provide genetic services including the ordering of genetic testing regardless of setting. For this to occur, GCs must first, at minimum, be legally recognized as HCPs. Our study also demonstrates that GCs outside of the Genetics Clinic may be the sole genetics expert in the workplace; 5/30 reported being the sole genetics expert in the workplace, while an additional 3/23 indicated multiple GCs in the workplace in the absence of other genetics‐trained professionals (e.g., MG or PhD). Therefore, 8/30 respondents are in a workplace in which GCs are the sole source of genetics expertise in the workplace. While this does not represent the majority, it is important to understand its implications as a model of genetic service delivery to consider in the future. From a liability standpoint, it is important to understand who is working with and supervising GCs as the RHCPs, in this context, serve as a means of mitigating the risk of harm to patients. Our survey results elicited a broad variety of responses for the titles of GC supervisors. However, the phrasing of the question may have elicited variable interpretations and not be directly representative of a RHCP, who could be held liable, among others, for patient harm in the given context. Considering the limited number of genetics professionals, having a single GC embedded in various settings (clinical and non‐clinical) is a way of increasing education, awareness, and access to genetics services for both patients and HCPs. In these positions, GCs often provide education and consultation to other HCPs and trainees, increasing general awareness of basic and complex genetics concepts. Such a model could also increase appropriate referrals to a Genetics Clinic for comprehensive genetics evaluation by an MG (Cook et al., 2021 ). 5.4. Implications for the legal recognition of GCs and regulation as healthcare professionals in Canada Hiring requirements for GCs in Canada (except for Manitoba) are established at the institutional level. Most require MS/MSc‐level training at an accredited Genetic Counseling training program with board eligible/board certified or active candidate status. While Genetics services often require Board certification or eligibility at the time of hire, there is no means of ensuring that this practice is consistent across all GC employers. Individuals using the title of GC without the appropriate training, as outlined by the CAGC and CBGC, remains a possibility (Patrinos et al., 2020 ). As it is, there are low levels of awareness of GCs in medicine, let alone in the general public. Employers who are unfamiliar with the necessary training and competencies for GCs may, therefore, unwittingly hire an unqualified individual in the absence of legal requirements. Taking into consideration increasing demands for genetics services, increasing GC positions outside of the Genetics Clinic, and a proportion of GCs being the sole genetics experts in the workplace; the potential for title misuse, and subsequent public harm, could potentially become a larger threat with time. In order to protect GC titles, GCs need to be legally recognized as HCPs across the country. Risk for patient harm and liability are also factors to consider. Our study demonstrates that GCs outside of the Genetics Clinic, including non‐clinical roles, can impact patient care and management. Considering GCs in these positions are working with high levels of professional autonomy, there is potential risk for patient harm if these roles are performed by an unqualified individual. Based on responses, the potential risks for patient harm are likely similar to previously described instances of patient harm (Bensend et al., 2014 ; Fox et al., 2016 ; Kelley, & Office of Policy, Research, and Regulatory Reform, 2013 ; NSGC, 2016 ), although further research in this area is needed. In the absence of support, genetic testing arranged by a non‐genetics‐trained HCP can result in inappropriate testing and misinterpretation of results (Miller et al., 2014 ). However, embedding a GC in a non‐genetics clinical service or at the laboratory level can reduce the likelihood of such events (Cook et al., 2021 ). Yet, if patient harm were to occur in this context, both the RHCP and the GC could be legally liable. The supervising non‐genetics RHCP could potentially be subject to medical malpractice secondary to inadequate training in genetics (Lambert et al., 2021 ), and GCs could be held liable by other means of the law, including potential liability for the illegal practice of medicine (Patrinos et al., 2020 ). In this context, two issues remain. First, the GC must still rely on the non‐genetics trained RHCP to order genetic testing. Our results demonstrate that GCs working outside of the Genetics Clinic are working with high levels of professional autonomy within their scope of practice, as defined by the CAGC and CBGC. GCs have the necessary skillset to facilitate the expansion of genetics service delivery across medical specialties while alleviating the burden from non‐genetics RHCPs. Second, the RHCP is ultimately liable for the possible diagnosis and subsequent medical management related to the genetic test they prescribed, which they may not be comfortable with despite the involvement of a GC. Regulation of GCs, including a clearly defined scope of practice, could provide clarity with regard to legal responsibility in the provision of genetic services. Legislative frameworks should be considered to set quality standards and reflect the competencies of HCPs, in a way that is sustainable for our public health system—starting with the legal recognition of GCs as HCPs. Potential frameworks, while beyond the scope of the current study, have been proposed by our group elsewhere (Lambert et al., 2021 ; Patrinos et al., 2020 ). 5.5. Limitations and future directions The current study is the first mixed methods study, to our knowledge, to explore the roles and practices of Canadian GCs in a variety of positions outside of the traditional setting (Medical Genetics Clinic), and was, therefore, intended to be general and exploratory in nature. Limitations of our study include small sample size and use of an unvalidated survey. Invitations were sent to CAGC members and advertised on GenCOUNSEL social media accounts, meaning that additional eligible GCs may not have had the opportunity to participate. Not all provinces/regions participated in this study, nor did any GCs practicing in neurogenetics participate in this study; implying that not all possible positions in Canada were represented. Due to these limitations, our results did not allow for statistical analysis and are not generalizable. In addition, use of an unvalidated survey, wherein definitions and clarifications were not provided for each item, allowed for multiple interpretations. A survey‐based modality for exploratory studies is not ideal as it often results in shorter, less nuanced responses compared to a qualitative interview‐based study. Future research could involve targeted interviews of GCs in specific positions outlined within our study (e.g., laboratory‐based GCs, research GCs, industry GCs, etc.) or studies on the perspective of supervising RHCPs. Our results are consistent with previous data with regard to risk for patient harm in genetic counseling practice in the traditional setting, and provide additional factors to consider for future evaluations of risk for patient harm associated with genetic counseling practice in different workplace settings. Nuance in the workflows and supervisory structures were not captured. Future investigations could involve comparing the risk of patient harm in GC practice within and outside of the Genetics Clinic. Moreover, the study did not address GC positions that do not involve patient‐related data. The results of this study, therefore, do not provide a comprehensive picture of all GC positions outside of the Genetics Clinic in Canada, and the ethico‐legal implications of such positions remain to be explored. 6. CONCLUSION The current study sought to provide a baseline in the landscape of genetic counseling practice outside of the Genetics Clinic in Canada, and its results serve as a necessary first step toward future investigations in the field. GCs outside of the Genetics Clinic across Canada are working not only well within their scope of practice, if not below when considering the large number of respondents citing administrative tasks, as defined by the CAGC and CBGC, but also exhibit a unique skillset that can be applied to novel roles. As GCs can be the sole genetics experts in the workplace, title protection is of great importance in order to ensure standards and the protection of the public. Current legislation for legal liability is incongruent with the skillset of HCPs involved in the provision of genetic services outside of the Genetics Clinic and will continue to limit patient access. These results further support the value of GCs translatable skillset in positions beyond the Genetics Clinic and denote a mechanism to effectively increase access to appropriate genetic testing to address continuously increasing waitlists amidst a shortage of genetics‐trained professionals both within and outside of the Genetics Clinic. The results of the current study provide evidence in support of legal recognition and regulation of GCs as HCPs. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS Samantha K. Rojas was involved in project conceptualization and development, data curation, project administration, data analysis, and writing. Dr. Ma''n H. Zawati was involved in the conceptualization, methodology, supervision, writing, review, and editing. Shelin Adam was involved in methodology, qualitative data analysis, writing, review, and editing. Dr. Alison M. Elliott was involved in conceptualization, writing, review, and editing. All authors confirm that they had full access to all the data in the study and take responsibility for the integrity of the data and accuracy of the data analysis. All of the authors gave final approval of this version to be published and agree to be accountable for all aspects of the work in ensuring that questions related to the accuracy or integrity of any part of the work are appropriately investigated and resolved. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT Samantha K. Rojas, Shelin Adam, Ma''n Zawati, and Alison Elliott declare that they have no conflict of interest. ETHICS STATEMENT Human studies and informed consent: Approval to conduct this human subject research was obtained by McGill University Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences Institutional Review Board. All procedures followed were in accordance with the ethical standards of the responsible committee on human experimentation. Informed consent was obtained for individuals who voluntarily completed the online survey and submitted their responses. Animal studies: No non‐human animal studies were carried out by the authors for this article. Supporting information Appendix S1 JGC4-34-0-s001.docx (30.9KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This study was completed in partial fulfillment of the requirements for the first author''s Master of Science degree in genetic counseling from McGill University. We thank Ms. Jennifer Fitzpatrick, Program Director, for advice on survey design. This work was supported by the GenCOUNSEL study. The GenCOUNSEL Study is led by Alison M. Elliott, Jehanine Austin, Bartha Knoppers, and Larry D. Lynd with Project Manager Alivia Dey, and includes the following co‐investigators: Shelin Adam, Nick Bansback, Patricia Birch, Lorne Clarke, Nick Dragojlovic, Jan Friedman, Debby Lambert, Daryl Pullman, Alice Virani, Wyeth Wasserman, and Ma''n Zawati. GenCOUNSEL was funded through the Large Scale Applied Research Project (LSARP) Genome Canada competition with co‐funding from: Canada Institute for Health Research (CIHR), Genome BC, Genome Quebec, Provincial Health Services Authority, BC Children''s Hospital Foundation and BC Women''s Hospital Foundation. Ma''n H. Zawati would like to acknowledge the funding provided by the FRQS through its J1 Career Award Program. Rojas, S. K. , Adam, S. ; Elliott, A. M. , & Zawati, M. H. (2025). Genetic counselors outside of the genetics clinic: Roles, practices, and ethico‐legal implications in light of lagging legal recognition across Canada. Journal of Genetic Counseling